// Draft summaries, written with AI, for verses that have no hand-written
// summary in curation.ts. Each is a short reading of the verse in our own
// words, drafted from the public-domain Sanskrit and the verse's concepts.
// Prabhupada's translation and purport were never shown to the AI. A local
// script compared each draft with the translation only to reject wording that
// came too close; none shares more than five consecutive words with it.
//
// The verse panel marks these as drafts. To accept one, move it into the
// verse's entry in curation.ts (which always wins) after checking it, and
// delete it here.
export const draftSummaries: Record<string, string> = {
  '1.2': "Sanjaya reports that Duryodhana, seeing the Pandava army drawn up for battle, goes to his teacher Drona to speak. His first move reveals the anxiety beneath the king's confident front as the war story opens.",
  '1.3': "Duryodhana points Drona to the vast Pandava army, noting it was arranged by Drupada's son, Drona's own clever student. The remark carries a barb, hinting that Drona trained the man now leading forces against him.",
  '1.4': "Duryodhana begins listing the great archers on the Pandava side, equal to Bhima and Arjuna in battle, starting with Yuyudhana, Virata and Drupada. His roll call betrays how formidable he finds the opposition.",
  '1.5': "Continuing his survey, Duryodhana names more powerful Pandava allies, including Dhrishtaketu, Chekitana, the king of Kashi, Purujit, Kuntibhoja and Shaibya. The growing list shows his mounting unease about the strength facing him.",
  '1.6': "Duryodhana completes the Pandava roll with Subhadra's son Abhimanyu and the sons of Draupadi, all great chariot warriors. The list underscores the depth of talent and family loyalty on the opposing side.",
  '1.7': "Duryodhana turns to his own army, telling Drona he will name its chief commanders for his information. Addressing Drona as best of the twice-born, he subtly reminds his teacher of his brahmin standing amid warrior affairs.",
  '1.8': "Duryodhana names his leading warriors: Drona himself, Bhishma, Karna, Kripa, Ashvatthama, Vikarna and the son of Somadatta. Placing Drona first, he seeks to flatter his teacher and secure his loyalty.",
  '1.9': "Duryodhana adds that many other heroes, armed with every kind of weapon and expert in war, are ready to give their lives for his sake. Unwittingly, his words foreshadow how many will die for his ambition.",
  '1.10': "Duryodhana weighs his own army, guarded by Bhishma, against the Pandava force guarded by Bhima. The word he uses for his strength can mean either boundless or inadequate, and that ambiguity mirrors his wavering confidence.",
  '1.11': "Duryodhana orders all his commanders to hold their assigned positions at every entry point and, above all, to protect Bhishma. His whole plan rests on keeping the elder grandsire safe as the army's mainstay.",
  '1.12': "To lift Duryodhana's spirits, the aged grandsire Bhishma roars like a lion and blows his conch with great force. The elder's gesture signals that battle is near and that he stands with the Kaurava side.",
  '1.13': "Following Bhishma's call, conches, kettledrums, tabors, drums and horns suddenly sound together across the Kaurava ranks, raising a tremendous uproar. The din marks the formal opening of hostilities.",
  '1.14': "Sanjaya turns to the Pandava side, where Krishna and Arjuna, seated on a great chariot yoked to white horses, sound their divine conches. Krishna's presence on that chariot quietly hints at where victory lies.",
  '1.15': "Krishna, master of the senses, blows his conch Panchajanya, Arjuna sounds Devadatta, and Bhima, famed for mighty deeds, blows the great conch Paundra. Each named conch shows the Pandava leaders answering the challenge with confidence.",
  '1.16': "King Yudhishthira, son of Kunti, sounds his conch Anantavijaya, while Nakula and Sahadeva blow Sughosha and Manipushpaka. With this, all five Pandava brothers have joined the call to battle.",
  '1.17': "Sanjaya names more Pandava allies who blow their conches: the master archer king of Kashi, the great chariot fighter Shikhandi, Dhrishtadyumna, Virata and the unconquered Satyaki. The roll steadily builds the scale of the moment.",
  '1.18': "Drupada, the sons of Draupadi and the strong-armed Abhimanyu each sound their own conches, Sanjaya tells the king. Every Pandava leader answers the challenge, completing a unified show of resolve.",
  '1.19': "The roar of the Pandava conches echoes through earth and sky and tears at the hearts of Dhritarashtra's sons. Sanjaya's report hints to the blind king that his side is already shaken.",
  '1.20': "Seeing the Kaurava ranks in position as weapons are about to fly, Arjuna, whose banner bears Hanuman, raises his bow and turns to speak to Krishna. The scene narrows from two armies to the dialogue at the Gita's heart.",
  '1.21': "Arjuna asks Krishna, the infallible one, to drive his chariot into the space between the two armies. The Lord accepts the role of charioteer and follows His friend's request, showing His affection for His devotee.",
  '1.22': "Arjuna explains that he wants to look over those standing ready and eager for war, so he can see whom he must fight in this great contest. His wish to see the enemy sets up the crisis to come.",
  '1.23': "Arjuna says he wants to observe those who have gathered to fight in order to please the wicked-minded son of Dhritarashtra. At this point he still sees the battle as a just stand against Duryodhana's wrongdoing.",
  '1.24': "Sanjaya relates that Krishna, addressed by Arjuna, places the finest of chariots between the two armies. With this simple act the Lord sets the stage for the teaching that will follow.",
  '1.25': "Stopping the chariot before Bhishma, Drona and the other rulers, Krishna tells Arjuna to look at the assembled Kurus. By calling him Partha, son of Pritha, Krishna gently stirs the family feelings that are about to surface.",
  '1.26': "Standing between the two armies, Arjuna notices relatives and loved ones on both sides, from elders such as grandfathers to friends and sons. Seeing his own kin arrayed for battle turns the war into a personal tragedy.",
  '1.27': "Seeing all his relatives arrayed for battle, Arjuna is flooded with pity and, sinking into sorrow, begins to speak. His tender feelings are genuine, yet they soon cloud his sense of duty.",
  '1.28': "Arjuna tells Krishna that the sight of his own people gathered and eager to fight has overwhelmed him. This opens his lament, as attachment to kin begins to overpower his resolve.",
  '1.29': "Arjuna describes his body giving way: his limbs grow weak, his mouth dries up, he trembles and his hair stands on end. Grief has taken physical hold of the greatest archer of his age.",
  '1.30': "Arjuna says his bow Gandiva is slipping from his grip, his skin is burning, and his mind is spinning so that he cannot stand steady. The collapse shows how attachment can paralyze even great strength.",
  '1.31': "Arjuna tells Krishna he sees only ill omens and can find no good in killing his own kinsmen in battle. His confusion now colors how he reads both signs and consequences.",
  '1.32': "Arjuna declares he has no desire for victory, kingdom or comforts, asking Krishna what use rule, pleasure or even life would be. This renunciation is born of grief, which Krishna will soon address.",
  '1.33': "Arjuna reflects that power and pleasure are worth seeking only to share with loved ones, yet those same loved ones now face him in battle, prepared to lose their fortunes and lives. Victory would leave him nothing to enjoy.",
  '1.34': "Arjuna widens his gaze from teachers and elders to every kind of kin, including in-laws and grandsons. The sheer range of family ties facing him in battle deepens his grief and his reluctance to fight.",
  '1.35': "Arjuna insists he would not strike his kinsmen even if they tried to kill him. Sovereignty over the whole cosmos would not tempt him, so an earthly kingdom means even less.",
  '1.36': "Arjuna asks what joy could come from killing Dhritarashtra's sons, arguing that sin would cling to the Pandavas even though these kinsmen are aggressors. He tries to give his reluctance a moral foundation.",
  '1.37': "Arjuna concludes that he and his brothers should not kill their own relatives, the sons of Dhritarashtra, asking how anyone could be happy after slaying family. His grief is hardening into a firm refusal to fight.",
  '1.38': "Arjuna acknowledges that his opponents, their minds overcome by greed, fail to see the wrong in destroying a family or betraying friends. He contrasts their blindness with his own awareness of the harm.",
  '1.39': "Arjuna asks why he and his brothers, who clearly see the evil of destroying a dynasty, should not turn back from this sin. He argues that understanding the consequences should lead them to hold back.",
  '1.40': "Arjuna argues that when a family is destroyed its age-old traditions perish, and once *dharma* is lost, lawlessness overtakes the whole family. He fears war will unravel the moral order that holds society together.",
  '1.41': "Arjuna continues that when lawlessness spreads, the family's women may go astray, and the social order becomes confused. He voices the traditional view that a stable society depends on stable family lines.",
  '1.42': "Arjuna says such social confusion drags both the family and its destroyers toward hell, while their ancestors fall because the ritual offerings of food and water to them stop. He sees the harm reaching even past generations.",
  '1.43': "Arjuna laments that through the misdeeds of those who ruin families and cause social confusion, both community duties and long-standing family traditions are wiped out. He foresees damage lasting far beyond the battlefield.",
  '1.44': "Arjuna tells Krishna he has heard that people whose family traditions have been destroyed dwell in hell for an indefinite time. He calls on received teaching to support his refusal to fight.",
  '1.45': "Arjuna cries out in dismay that they have resolved to commit a terrible sin, prepared to kill their own kin out of greed for royal pleasures. He now judges his own side's motives harshly.",
  '1.46': "Arjuna concludes he would be better off if Dhritarashtra's armed sons killed him in battle while he stood unarmed and unresisting. His despair reaches its lowest point, setting the stage for Krishna's teaching.",
  '2.1': "Sanjaya describes Arjuna overcome by pity, his eyes brimming with tears and his heart sunk in despair, as Krishna, slayer of the demon Madhu, begins to speak. The second chapter opens with the teacher stepping in.",
  '2.2': "Krishna, the Supreme Lord, asks Arjuna where this weakness has come from at such a critical hour. He calls it unworthy of a noble person, since it leads neither to higher worlds nor to honor.",
  '2.3': "Krishna urges Arjuna not to give in to cowardice, which does not suit him. He tells him to throw off this small-hearted faintness and stand up, calling him scorcher of foes to rekindle his courage.",
  '2.4': "Arjuna asks Krishna how he can shoot arrows at Bhishma and Drona, elders who deserve his worship. His question shows that reverence for elders now stands in the way of his warrior duty.",
  '2.5': "Arjuna reasons that begging for food would be preferable to slaying his revered gurus. Any wealth or pleasure gained by killing them, he feels, would be tainted with their blood, so honoring elders outweighs victory.",
  '2.6': "Arjuna admits he cannot tell which is better, winning or losing, since killing the sons of Dhritarashtra standing before him would leave him no wish to live. His confusion is now complete.",
  '2.7': "Arjuna admits that weakness has clouded his nature and left him unsure of his duty. He asks Krishna to tell him clearly what is best and surrenders as His disciple, a turning point that begins the Gita's teaching.",
  '2.8': "Arjuna says he sees nothing that could remove the grief drying up his senses, not even an unrivaled, prosperous kingdom on earth or rule over the gods. He recognizes that worldly power cannot heal his sorrow.",
  '2.9': "Sanjaya reports that after speaking, Arjuna tells Krishna, Govinda, that he will not fight, and then falls silent. His surrender and his refusal sit side by side, awaiting Krishna's reply.",
  '2.10': "Sanjaya says Krishna, as if smiling, speaks to the grieving Arjuna there between the two armies. The gentle smile signals the Lord's calm mastery of the situation and His affection for His friend.",
  '2.11': "Krishna points out that Arjuna talks like a scholar yet sorrows over something that calls for no sorrow. Those with true insight, He says, lament for no one, whether their body lives or has died.",
  '2.12': "Krishna declares that He, Arjuna and all the assembled kings have always existed and will go on existing in the future. The verse establishes that both the Lord and each individual soul are eternal and distinct.",
  '2.13': "Krishna compares death to the changes within a single lifetime: the self that moves from childhood through youth into old age simply moves on to a new body. Knowing this, the wise stay composed when death comes.",
  '2.15': "Krishna teaches that someone whom the ups and downs of joy and sorrow can no longer shake, and who meets both with the same calm, is ready for deathless life. Inner steadiness prepares the soul for liberation.",
  '2.16': "Krishna states a basic principle: what is temporary has no enduring being, and what is truly real never stops existing. Those who see things as they are recognize this difference between the passing body and the lasting self.",
  '2.17': "Krishna tells Arjuna to recognize that the conscious presence spread throughout the body can never be destroyed. Nothing and no one has the power to bring an end to this unchanging self.",
  '2.18': "Krishna explains that bodies are bound to end, while the embodied self is eternal, indestructible and beyond measure. For that reason He tells Arjuna to fight, since grief over perishable bodies should not stop him from his duty.",
  '2.19': "Krishna says that both the person who sees the self as a killer and the one who sees it as a victim are mistaken. The true self takes no life and loses none, which answers Arjuna's dread of killing.",
  '2.21': "Krishna asks how a person who knows the self to be eternal, unborn, unchanging and indestructible could kill anyone or cause anyone's death. Real knowledge of the soul dissolves Arjuna's moral panic.",
  '2.22': "Krishna offers an image from daily life: as people set aside worn clothing and dress in fresh garments, the embodied self leaves an aged body and takes up another. Death is simply a change of covering.",
  '2.23': "Krishna explains that the self lies beyond the reach of every destructive force: blades cannot pierce it, flames cannot consume it, water cannot soak it and wind cannot parch it. Material elements affect only the body.",
  '2.24': "Krishna restates that the self cannot be cut, burned, soaked or dried. It is eternal, able to dwell in every kind of body, fixed, unmoving and timeless, so its nature stays the same whatever happens to the body.",
  '2.25': "Krishna adds that the self cannot be perceived by the senses, grasped by thought or altered in any way. Once Arjuna understands this, his grief over the body loses its basis.",
  '2.26': "Krishna argues that even if Arjuna believed the self is constantly born and constantly dies, he still should not grieve. He meets Arjuna on materialist terms to show that sorrow is unjustified either way.",
  '2.27': "Krishna points out that death is certain for whatever is born, and birth is certain for whatever dies. Since this cycle cannot be avoided, grieving over it serves no purpose.",
  '2.28': "Krishna notes that living beings cannot be seen before birth, become visible for a time, and pass out of sight again at death. Their bodily appearance is a brief middle chapter, so heavy mourning makes little sense.",
  '2.29': "Krishna remarks that the self strikes people as astonishing: some glimpse it with awe, some describe it in amazement, some listen in wonder, and still most fail to grasp it. Real understanding of the soul is rare.",
  '2.30': "Krishna sums up His teaching on the soul: the one who lives within every body is eternal and beyond harm. For that reason Arjuna has no cause to mourn for any creature.",
  '2.31': "Krishna now appeals to Arjuna's own calling as a warrior, saying he should not waver, since nothing is better for a *kshatriya* than a righteous fight. A practical argument from duty now supports the spiritual one.",
  '2.32': "Krishna tells Arjuna that a righteous war arriving unsought is a stroke of good fortune for a warrior, offering an open path to the heavenly realms. He presents the battle as an opportunity to welcome.",
  '2.33': "Krishna warns that if Arjuna refuses this righteous war, he will abandon both his duty and his good name and incur sin. Avoiding his responsibility would itself be a wrong.",
  '2.34': "Krishna warns that the world would talk of Arjuna's shame for a long time to come. For someone held in high esteem, He notes, such lasting disgrace hurts more than dying.",
  '2.35': "Krishna says the great chariot warriors will assume Arjuna withdrew from battle out of fear, and those who once admired him will think little of him. His withdrawal would be read as cowardice.",
  '2.36': "Krishna adds that Arjuna's enemies will say many cruel and unfit things, mocking his ability. He asks Arjuna what could be more painful than that kind of ridicule.",
  '2.37': "Krishna points out that if Arjuna falls he will reach heaven, and if he wins he will enjoy the earth. Since either outcome serves him, Krishna urges him to rise and fight with firm resolve.",
  '2.38': "Krishna instructs Arjuna to fight while treating pleasure and pain, gain and loss, victory and defeat as equal. Acting with that evenness, he will incur no sin, which introduces the spirit of detached action.",
  '2.39': "Krishna marks a shift: having described the self analytically, He will now explain the same insight as practice, the yoga of intelligence. Working with this understanding releases a person from the binding effects of *karma*.",
  '2.40': "Krishna reassures Arjuna that nothing invested in this spiritual path goes to waste and no setback undoes it. Even a small amount of genuine practice shields a person from the gravest danger, the cycle of birth and death.",
  '2.41': "Krishna contrasts two minds: the determined seeker's intelligence is fixed on one aim, while the undecided person's thinking splits in countless directions. Single-pointed resolve, ultimately centered on the Lord, marks the spiritual path.",
  '2.42': "Krishna criticizes people of shallow understanding who delight in the flowery promises of Vedic ritual and insist there is nothing higher. Driven by desire and aiming for heaven, they treat religion as a route to pleasure and power.",
  '2.43': "Krishna describes such people as full of worldly desire, fixed on reaching heaven and drawn to elaborate rites that promise good births, enjoyment and wealth. Their religious practice becomes a means to material gain.",
  '2.44': "Krishna explains that people attached to enjoyment and power, their minds carried off by such promises, cannot develop the resolute intelligence needed for absorption in the Divine. Desire scatters the focus that spiritual life requires.",
  '2.45': "Krishna observes that the Vedic rituals mostly operate within the three *gunas* and urges Arjuna to move beyond them. He should stand above opposing pairs, rooted in pure goodness, free of worry over acquiring and keeping, and grounded in the self.",
  '2.46': "Krishna uses an image of water: someone beside a vast lake has no need for a small well. In the same way, a person who truly knows the Absolute already gains every benefit the Vedic rites can offer.",
  '2.48': "Krishna asks Arjuna to act while anchored in yoga, letting go of clinging and staying balanced whether things succeed or fail. He defines yoga itself as this inner evenness.",
  '2.49': "Krishna says action done for its rewards ranks far below action guided by spiritual intelligence, and urges Arjuna to take shelter in that intelligence. Those who work only for results, He says, are small-minded and to be pitied.",
  '2.50': "Krishna explains that a person acting with this devoted intelligence leaves behind both good and bad karmic results here and now. He urges Arjuna to practice yoga, which He calls real expertise in how one acts.",
  '2.51': "Krishna says thoughtful people who act with this devoted understanding and release their claim on results escape the chain of rebirth. They reach a state untouched by sorrow, the destination of detached service.",
  '2.52': "Krishna tells Arjuna that once his understanding moves past the tangled thicket of confusion, he will lose interest in what he has heard or might still hear about ritual rewards. Clarity replaces the pull of such promises.",
  '2.53': "Krishna says that when Arjuna's intelligence stops being tossed about by competing scriptural claims and rests unmoving in deep absorption (*samadhi*), he will have reached yoga. Stable insight is the goal of the practice.",
  '2.54': "Arjuna asks Krishna to describe a person of steady wisdom who is fixed in spiritual absorption: how such a person speaks, sits and moves. His question opens the famous portrait of the *sthita-prajna*.",
  '2.55': "Krishna answers that a person is called steady in wisdom when they let go of every desire arising in the mind and find contentment within the self alone. Inner satisfaction takes the place of craving.",
  '2.57': "Krishna says that someone without clinging attachment anywhere, who neither celebrates good fortune nor resents misfortune, is firmly established in wisdom. Evenness toward pleasant and unpleasant events is a sign of real insight.",
  '2.58': "Krishna compares the person of steady wisdom to a tortoise drawing in its limbs: such a person can withdraw the senses from their objects at will. Mastery over the senses marks settled insight.",
  '2.59': "Krishna notes that abstaining from sense objects may hold them at a distance, yet the taste for them lingers. That inner taste fades only when a person experiences the Supreme, a higher joy that ends the craving.",
  '2.60': "Krishna cautions that the senses are powerful and restless enough to drag away the mind of even a learned person who is making real effort. Self-control is never something to take lightly.",
  '2.61': "Krishna teaches that the practitioner should rein in every sense and stay disciplined, with Krishna as the highest goal (*mat-para*). Sense mastery achieved by absorbing the mind in Him makes wisdom firm.",
  '2.64': "Krishna explains that a self-controlled person who deals with sense objects while free from attraction and aversion attains inner clarity and grace (*prasada*). Engagement without craving brings peace.",
  '2.65': "Krishna says that in this serenity all sorrows come to an end, and the intelligence of one whose mind is clear soon becomes firmly settled. Peace and steady insight grow together.",
  '2.66': "Krishna traces a chain: without a link to the Divine, a person lacks settled understanding and inner focus; lacking focus, they cannot find peace, and lacking peace, real happiness stays out of reach.",
  '2.67': "Krishna likens the mind to a boat and the wandering senses to wind: when the mind trails after whichever sense is active, understanding is pushed off course. A single unchecked sense can derail wisdom.",
  '2.68': "Krishna concludes that one whose senses are fully held back from their objects has firmly established wisdom. The verse closes His argument for sense mastery as the foundation of insight.",
  '2.69': "Krishna uses a paradox of day and night: the disciplined sage stays awake to the spiritual truth that others sleep through, while the worldly concerns that keep others busy are darkness to him. Their priorities are reversed.",
  '2.70': "Krishna offers the image of the ocean, which stays calm and full even as rivers keep flowing into it. A person who lets desires enter without being stirred finds peace, while someone forever chasing desires stays restless.",
  '2.71': "Krishna says a person who sets aside cravings and moves through life without longing, without claiming ownership and without self-importance comes to peace. Releasing the sense of me and mine brings lasting calm.",
  '2.72': "Krishna concludes that this is the spiritual state (*brahmi-sthiti*); whoever reaches it is no longer bewildered, and remaining in it even at death, attains liberation in the Absolute. It completes His portrait of steady wisdom.",
  '3.1': "Arjuna, puzzled, asks Krishna why he is being urged into a terrible battle if Krishna regards understanding as superior to action. The question opens Chapter 3's inquiry into whether one should act or withdraw.",
  '3.2': "Arjuna admits that Krishna's teaching seems mixed and leaves him confused. He asks for one clear, settled instruction that will lead him to his highest good, voicing every seeker's need for direction.",
  '3.3': "Krishna replies that He has long taught two paths to steadiness: the path of knowledge for contemplatives and the path of selfless action for practitioners of yoga. Both aim at the same goal.",
  '3.4': "Krishna explains that simply avoiding work does not bring freedom from karmic reaction, and outward renunciation alone does not bring perfection. Inner purification through rightly directed action is required.",
  '3.6': "Krishna warns that someone who restrains the bodily senses while the mind keeps dwelling on sense objects is deceiving himself. Outer stillness without inner control is a form of hypocrisy.",
  '3.7': "In contrast, Krishna praises the person who governs the senses with the mind and engages in *karma-yoga* without attachment. Such disciplined, detached work is superior to artificial withdrawal.",
  '3.8': "Krishna tells Arjuna to perform his prescribed duty, since action is better than inaction. Even maintaining the body is impossible without work, so complete idleness is neither practical nor virtuous.",
  '3.9': "Krishna teaches that work done as an offering, *yajna*, frees one, while all other work binds a person to this world. Arjuna should therefore act for that higher purpose, free of attachment.",
  '3.10': "Krishna recounts that at creation the progenitor Prajapati brought forth beings along with sacrifice and blessed them to prosper through it. Offering was built into the order of life from the start.",
  '3.11': "The verse describes a cycle of mutual care: humans please the gods through sacrifice, and the gods in turn sustain humans. Through this reciprocity both reach the highest welfare.",
  '3.12': "Krishna explains that the gods, pleased by sacrifice, provide life's necessities. Whoever enjoys these gifts without offering anything back is effectively a thief, ignoring the debt owed to the giver.",
  '3.13': "Krishna says the virtuous who eat what remains after an offering are freed from wrongdoing, while those who cook only for themselves consume sin. Offering food first purifies daily life.",
  '3.14': "Krishna traces a chain of dependence: beings live on food, food comes from rain, rain arises from sacrifice, and sacrifice springs from prescribed action. Human work sustains the cosmic order.",
  '3.15': "The chain continues: prescribed action comes from the Vedas, which come from the imperishable Supreme. Thus the all-pervading Absolute is always present in acts of sacrifice, linking duty to the Divine.",
  '3.16': "Krishna warns that whoever refuses to take part in this cycle of offering, living only for sense pleasure, lives a sinful and wasted life. Self-centered living breaks the order that sustains everyone.",
  '3.17': "Krishna notes an exception: one who delights in the self, is fulfilled within, and is fully content has no further duty to perform. Inner realization lifts a person beyond obligatory work.",
  '3.18': "Such a self-satisfied person gains nothing by acting and loses nothing by not acting, and depends on no being for any purpose. The verse describes freedom from all worldly need.",
  '3.20': "Krishna cites King Janaka and other sages who reached perfection through action itself. Arjuna too should act, if only to guide and protect society, the ideal of *loka-sangraha*.",
  '3.21': "Krishna observes that ordinary people imitate whatever leaders do, and the world follows the standards they set. Those in positions of influence carry responsibility for the example they give.",
  '3.22': "Krishna points to Himself: there is nothing in all three worlds He must do and nothing He lacks, yet He still engages in action. The Supreme acts freely, out of care for the world.",
  '3.23': "Krishna explains that if He ever stopped working tirelessly, people everywhere would follow His path into inaction. His own conduct sets the pattern humanity copies.",
  '3.24': "Krishna says that if He ceased acting, the worlds would fall into ruin, social order would collapse into confusion, and He would be the cause of harm to all beings. His example upholds dharma.",
  '3.25': "Krishna urges the wise to work just as energetically as the ignorant do, but without attachment, aiming at the welfare of the world. The difference lies in inner motive, while outward diligence stays the same.",
  '3.26': "Krishna advises the wise not to unsettle the minds of those attached to their work. Instead they should act with devotion and steadiness themselves, encouraging others to keep doing their duties.",
  '3.28': "Krishna explains that one who truly knows how the modes of nature and their actions operate sees that senses simply engage with sense objects. Seeing this, such a person does not become attached.",
  '3.29': "People bewildered by nature's modes cling to the actions those modes produce. Krishna advises the fully knowing not to disturb these slow-witted, partially informed people, but to lead them gradually.",
  '3.30': "Krishna asks Arjuna to dedicate all his actions to Him, with his mind fixed on the Self, free of desire and possessiveness, and to fight without anxiety. Action offered to Krishna becomes surrender.",
  '3.31': "Krishna promises that those who faithfully and without envy follow this teaching of His are released from the bondage of karma. Sincere trust in His instruction is itself liberating.",
  '3.32': "By contrast, those who resent this teaching and refuse to follow it are bewildered about all knowledge. Krishna says such people lose their way and fall into ruin.",
  '3.33': "Krishna points out that even a wise person behaves in line with his conditioned nature, because every creature follows its inborn tendencies. Forcible suppression accomplishes little, so nature has to be directed wisely.",
  '3.34': "Krishna warns that each sense harbors attraction and dislike toward its objects. Arjuna should not let these two forces rule him, since they block the road to spiritual progress.",
  '3.36': "Arjuna asks Krishna what force drives a person to commit wrong, even against his own will, as if pushed by compulsion. His question introduces the inner enemy Krishna will now expose.",
  '3.37': "Krishna identifies the culprit as desire, which turns into anger when frustrated. Born of the mode of passion, it is insatiable and deeply destructive, the real enemy of the soul in this world.",
  '3.38': "Krishna gives three images of how desire covers awareness: as smoke obscures fire, dust a mirror, and the womb an embryo. Each shows a different depth of covering over the living being.",
  '3.39': "Krishna says the knowledge of even a wise person is veiled by this constant enemy, desire, which burns like a fire that can never be satisfied. Feeding it only makes it grow.",
  '3.40': "Krishna reveals where desire takes up residence: the senses, mind and intellect. Working through them, it clouds knowledge and bewilders the embodied soul.",
  '3.41': "Krishna therefore instructs Arjuna to first regulate the senses and then destroy desire, the sinful force that ruins both theoretical knowledge and direct realization. Discipline begins at the senses.",
  '3.42': "Krishna describes a hierarchy: the senses are higher than matter, the mind higher than the senses, intelligence higher than the mind, and the soul higher still. Knowing this order empowers self-control.",
  '3.43': "Knowing the soul to be beyond intelligence, one should steady the mind through spiritual intelligence and conquer the formidable enemy called desire. Chapter 3 closes with this call to inner victory.",
  '4.1': "Krishna reveals that He first taught this imperishable yoga to the sun-god Vivasvan, who passed it to Manu, who taught King Ikshvaku. The wisdom has divine origin and a lineage of transmission.",
  '4.2': "Krishna explains that saintly kings understood this knowledge through such a disciplic chain, *parampara*. Over a long span of time the chain was broken and the teaching was lost.",
  '4.3': "Krishna says He is now teaching that same ancient yoga to Arjuna, because Arjuna is His devotee and friend. This supreme secret is opened through a relationship of devotion.",
  '4.4': "Arjuna asks how he can make sense of Krishna's claim, since Vivasvan was born long before Krishna. His question prompts Krishna to reveal His divine nature.",
  '4.5': "Krishna answers that both He and Arjuna have passed through many births. Krishna remembers them all, while Arjuna does not, highlighting the difference between the Lord and the individual soul.",
  '4.6': "Krishna explains that although He is unborn, unchanging and the Lord of all beings, He appears in this world by His own internal power, governing His nature rather than being governed by it.",
  '4.8': "Krishna declares that He appears age after age to protect the virtuous, destroy wrongdoers and re-establish dharma. His descents express care for the world and its moral order.",
  '4.9': "Krishna teaches that whoever truly understands the divine nature of His birth and activities is not reborn after leaving the body, and instead comes to Him. Knowing Krishna rightly brings liberation.",
  '4.10': "Krishna explains that countless people have reached loving union with him by letting go of craving, fear and anger. Thinking of him constantly and relying on him, they became purified through knowledge and austerity.",
  '4.11': "Krishna explains that He responds to people according to how they approach Him. Everyone, in every way, follows a path that ultimately leads to Him, and He reciprocates their surrender.",
  '4.12': "Krishna observes that people who want quick success in their work worship the gods, because in the human world results from such rituals come swiftly. Such worship serves material aims.",
  '4.13': "Krishna says He created the four social orders according to qualities and work. Although He is their maker, He remains the unchanging non-doer, untouched by the arrangement He set up.",
  '4.14': "Krishna explains that action does not stain Him, and He has no desire for its fruits. Whoever understands Him in this way is also not bound by karma.",
  '4.15': "Krishna reminds Arjuna that liberated souls of old understood the nature of action and still worked. Following that ancient example, Arjuna should take up his duty rather than withdraw from it.",
  '4.16': "Krishna admits that even the wise are confused about what action is and what inaction is. He promises to explain action clearly so that Arjuna may be freed from misfortune.",
  '4.17': "Krishna says one must understand three things: proper action, forbidden action and inaction. The way of action is deep and difficult to grasp, so careful discernment is needed.",
  '4.19': "The wise call a person learned when all his undertakings are free from selfish desire and his karmic reactions have been burned away in the fire of knowledge.",
  '4.20': "Having given up attachment to results, always content and dependent on nothing, such a person does nothing in the binding sense, even while fully engaged in activity.",
  '4.21': "Free from craving, with mind and self controlled and having let go of possessiveness, a person who acts only to maintain the body incurs no sinful reaction.",
  '4.22': "Krishna describes one content with whatever comes unsought, beyond dualities, free of envy and steady in success and failure. Such a person acts yet remains unbound.",
  '4.23': "For one free of attachment and liberated, whose mind rests in knowledge and who works as an offering, all karma dissolves completely. Action done as *yajna* leaves no residue.",
  '4.24': "The verse describes a vision in which the offering, the act of offering, the fire and the offerer are all seen as spiritual. One absorbed in such spiritual action reaches the spiritual goal.",
  '4.25': "Krishna begins listing kinds of sacrifice: some yogis worship the gods with offerings, while others offer the self into the fire of the Absolute. Sacrifice takes many forms.",
  '4.26': "Krishna continues: some offer hearing and the other senses into the fire of restraint, while others offer sound and other sense objects into the fire of the senses, regulating their use.",
  '4.27': "Others offer all the functions of the senses and the vital breath into the fire of self-control, kindled by knowledge. Inner discipline itself becomes a sacrifice.",
  '4.28': "Krishna names further sacrifices: giving wealth, austerity, yoga practice, scriptural study and pursuit of knowledge, undertaken by disciplined people bound by strict vows.",
  '4.29': "Krishna mentions yogis who work with the breath, merging inhalation and exhalation or suspending both, and others who restrict their eating. Every such disciplined practice, understood as sacrifice, washes away sin.",
  '4.30': "Krishna adds that some, controlling their eating, offer the life airs into themselves, and that all these knowers of sacrifice are purified of faults through their offerings.",
  '4.31': "Those who partake of the nectar left over from sacrifice reach the eternal Absolute. Krishna adds that without sacrifice one cannot be happy even in this world, let alone the next.",
  '4.32': "Krishna says these many forms of sacrifice are taught in the Vedas, and all arise from action. Understanding this, Arjuna will become free.",
  '4.33': "Krishna declares that sacrifice performed with knowledge is superior to material offerings, because all action finds its fulfillment in knowledge. Understanding gives every act its purpose.",
  '4.34': "Krishna instructs that this knowledge is learned by approaching a realized teacher with humility, sincere questions and service. Those who have seen the truth can then impart it.",
  '4.35': "Krishna promises that once Arjuna gains this knowledge he will never again be deluded, and he will see all beings as part of the Supreme, and therefore as connected with Krishna.",
  '4.36': "Krishna assures Arjuna that even the worst of sinners can cross the ocean of wrongdoing using the boat of spiritual knowledge. No past is beyond redemption.",
  '4.37': "Krishna compares knowledge to a blazing fire that reduces fuel to ash. In the same way, the fire of knowledge burns up the reactions of all karma.",
  '4.39': "Krishna teaches that a person of faith, devoted to the goal and with senses controlled, gains knowledge, and having gained it, quickly attains supreme peace. Faith opens the door.",
  '4.40': "Krishna cautions that lacking knowledge and faith leads to ruin, and that persistent doubt is the worst condition of all. A person consumed by suspicion finds no peace here and no hope beyond this life.",
  '4.41': "Krishna says actions do not bind one who has renounced their fruits through yoga, whose doubts are cut by knowledge and who is anchored in the self.",
  '4.42': "Krishna urges Arjuna to cut the doubt born of ignorance in his heart with the sword of knowledge, take shelter in yoga, and stand up to act. Chapter 4 ends with a call to rise.",
  '5.1': "Arjuna asks Krishna which is better, renouncing action or performing action in yoga, since Krishna seems to praise both. He wants one definite answer.",
  '5.2': "Krishna replies that both renunciation and devoted action lead to the highest good, but of the two, *karma-yoga* is better than giving up work. Action offered rightly is the safer path.",
  '5.3': "Krishna explains that a true renunciant is one who neither hates nor craves. Free of opposing pairs, such a person is easily released from bondage, whatever his outward station.",
  '5.4': "Krishna says only the immature call analytical knowledge and devotional action different paths. The wise know that one who properly practices either attains the fruit of both.",
  '5.5': "The state reached through analytical study is also reached through devoted action. Krishna says whoever sees these two as one sees things as they truly are.",
  '5.6': "Krishna explains that renunciation without devotional engagement leads only to distress, while the sage absorbed in yoga quickly reaches the Absolute. Withdrawal alone is hard and unfruitful.",
  '5.7': "One engaged in devotion, purified in heart, self-controlled, master of the senses and feeling kinship with all beings, is not tainted even while acting.",
  '5.8': "Krishna explains that a person who knows the truth, while seeing, eating, walking or breathing, recognizes that only the senses are meeting their objects. Such a person does not think of himself as the one acting.",
  '5.9': "Continuing, even while speaking, releasing, grasping, or opening and closing the eyes, the enlightened person remembers that the senses are simply moving among their objects, staying inwardly free.",
  '5.11': "Detached yogis engage every faculty, from body and intellect down to the bare senses, purely to purify themselves. Krishna shows that work done without attachment turns into a way of purifying the heart.",
  '5.12': "One who is steady in devotion and gives up the fruits of action attains lasting peace, while one driven by desire and attached to results becomes bound.",
  '5.13': "Krishna pictures the body as a city with nine gates in which the soul lives. Having given up all deeds inwardly, the disciplined resident stays content there, knowing he is not truly the one who acts or makes others act.",
  '5.14': "Krishna explains that the Lord does not create a person's sense of doership, their actions or the link between action and result. These are set in motion by one's own conditioned nature.",
  '5.15': "The all-pervading Lord does not take on anyone's sins or good deeds. Living beings are bewildered because their knowledge is covered by ignorance.",
  '5.16': "For those whose ignorance has been destroyed by knowledge, that knowledge shines like the sun and reveals the Supreme. Understanding dispels darkness and shows reality.",
  '5.17': "Those whose intellect, mind, faith and refuge are fixed in the Supreme, cleansed of impurity by knowledge, reach the state from which there is no return to rebirth.",
  '5.19': "Krishna says those whose minds rest in equality have conquered birth and death even in this life. Since the Absolute is flawless and equal to all, they are established in it.",
  '5.20': "The knower of the Absolute neither rejoices at gaining the pleasant nor grieves at meeting the unpleasant. Steady in intelligence and free of delusion, such a person abides in the spiritual.",
  '5.21': "One unattached to external sense contact finds happiness within. Absorbed in yoga with the Absolute, that person enjoys a joy that never ends.",
  '5.22': "Krishna teaches that enjoyments arising from contact between senses and objects begin and end, and they breed misery. Because of this, an intelligent person finds no joy in them.",
  '5.23': "Krishna says that whoever can withstand the surge of desire and anger here, before leaving the body, is a true yogi and is genuinely happy.",
  '5.24': "One whose happiness, delight and light are all within reaches *brahma-nirvana*, the peace of the spiritual, having become established in the Absolute.",
  '5.25': "Sages whose impurities are destroyed, whose doubts are cut away, who are self-controlled and devoted to the welfare of all beings attain liberation in the Absolute.",
  '5.26': "For renunciants free of desire and anger, with controlled minds and knowledge of the self, liberation in the Absolute is close at hand on every side.",
  '5.27': "Krishna outlines meditative practice: shutting out external objects, fixing the gaze between the brows, balancing the breath, and controlling senses, mind and intellect. The sage intent on liberation, free of desire, fear and anger, is truly free.",
  '5.28': "The sage who controls senses, mind and intellect, aims at liberation, and has given up desire, fear and anger is always free. This completes Krishna's sketch of meditation.",
  '5.29': "Krishna concludes that peace comes from knowing Him as the true enjoyer of all sacrifice and austerity, the great Lord of all worlds, and the friend of every being.",
  '6.1': "Krishna opens by defining the true renunciant and yogi as someone who does required work without depending on its results. Simply giving up ritual fires or stopping all activity does not earn either title.",
  '6.2': "Krishna tells Arjuna that renunciation and yoga are essentially the same, since no one becomes a yogi without first giving up self-centered plans and desires for personal gain.",
  '6.3': "Krishna marks two stages of practice. For someone beginning the climb toward yoga, disciplined work is the means; for one who has reached the summit, inner calm and the quieting of activity become the means.",
  '6.4': "A person is said to have reached the height of yoga when they no longer cling to sense objects or to their own actions, having released every self-centered intention born of desire.",
  '6.6': "Krishna explains that for one who has mastered the mind, the mind becomes a true ally, while for one who has not, that same mind acts like a hostile enemy. Self-control decides which role it plays.",
  '6.7': "Krishna describes the yogi who has mastered the mind and become tranquil as already reaching the Supersoul, the *Paramatma*. Such a person stays balanced whether facing heat or cold, joy or sorrow, respect or disgrace.",
  '6.8': "The steady yogi is satisfied by knowledge and direct realization, unshaken, with senses mastered, and sees a lump of earth, a stone and gold as equal in worth. Such a person is called established in yoga.",
  '6.9': "Krishna rates even higher the person who regards well-wishers, friends, enemies, neutral parties, mediators, the hostile, relatives, saints and sinners with the same even mind. Impartial vision marks advanced spiritual maturity.",
  '6.10': "Krishna begins his instructions on meditation: the yogi should keep the mind constantly engaged, staying alone in a secluded place, with thoughts and body controlled, free from craving and from the urge to possess.",
  '6.11': "Krishna advises choosing a clean spot for meditation and setting up a stable seat of moderate height, layered with sacred grass, deerskin and a cloth on top. A proper outer setting supports focus within.",
  '6.12': "Seated there, the practitioner fixes the mind on one point, restrains thought, senses and activity, and practices yoga to purify the heart. Krishna presents meditation as a process of inner cleansing.",
  '6.13': "Krishna instructs the meditator to sit upright and motionless, keeping the spine, neck and head in one line. The eyes should rest on the nose tip without looking around, since a still body helps quiet the mind.",
  '6.14': "Calm, fearless and firm in celibacy, the yogi should restrain the mind, fix it on Krishna and hold him as the highest goal. Krishna reveals that he himself is the true object of meditation.",
  '6.15': "By practicing steadily with a controlled mind, the yogi attains the supreme peace that ends material existence and abides in Krishna. Liberation is pictured as a positive state of fulfillment in him.",
  '6.16': "Krishna warns against extremes: yoga cannot succeed for someone who eats too much or too little, or who sleeps too much or too little. Balanced habits form the practical foundation for spiritual life.",
  '6.18': "When the disciplined mind settles in the self alone and no longer longs for any pleasure, the practitioner is said to be established in yoga. Steady inner focus and freedom from craving go together.",
  '6.19': "Krishna likens the disciplined mind of a meditating yogi to a lamp sheltered from the wind, whose flame stays perfectly still. The image captures the calm clarity that steady practice brings.",
  '6.20': "Krishna describes the perfected state in which the mind, restrained through practice, comes fully to rest, and the yogi perceives the self through the purified mind and finds deep contentment within.",
  '6.21': "In that state, the yogi knows a limitless happiness grasped by the purified intellect beyond the senses, and once established there never drifts from the truth. Spiritual joy far exceeds sensual pleasure.",
  '6.22': "Having gained this state, the yogi considers no other achievement greater, and even severe hardship cannot shake them. Krishna describes a fulfillment so complete that worldly loss loses its hold.",
  '6.23': "Krishna defines yoga as freedom from all contact with suffering and urges that it be practiced with firm conviction and a heart that never grows discouraged. Perseverance is essential to success.",
  '6.24': "The practitioner should give up completely all desires born of mental scheming and use the mind to rein in the senses from every side. Since desire starts as thought, control begins there too.",
  '6.25': "Krishna stresses patient, gradual progress: little by little, the mind should be quieted by an intellect held firm with resolve, settled in the self and kept from dwelling on anything else.",
  '6.26': "Krishna offers a practical method for meditation: each time the fickle mind drifts away, gently bring it back and place it under the self's control. Patience with this constant returning is the core of the practice.",
  '6.27': "The highest happiness comes to the yogi whose mind is peaceful, whose passion is stilled and who is free of sin, having realized spiritual identity, the state called *brahma-bhuta*. Inner calm opens the way to lasting joy.",
  '6.28': "Steady in practice and cleansed of impurity, the yogi easily tastes the boundless happiness of contact with the Absolute. Krishna presents spiritual bliss as the natural fruit of persistent, purified practice.",
  '6.29': "The yogi united in yoga sees the Supreme Self present in every being and every being resting in that Self, viewing all with equal vision. This insight dissolves prejudice and grounds compassion.",
  '6.30': "Krishna promises that whoever sees him everywhere and sees everything within him is never separated from him, and he never leaves that person's sight. The verse describes a constant, mutual bond of loving awareness.",
  '6.31': "The yogi who worships Krishna as the one Lord dwelling in all beings remains in Krishna whatever their outward circumstances. Devotion to the indwelling Lord sustains an unbroken spiritual connection.",
  '6.32': "Krishna names as the highest yogi one who, using their own experience as the measure, feels the joys and sorrows of all beings equally. Empathy rooted in spiritual vision is the peak of this practice.",
  '6.33': "Arjuna replies that he cannot see how the yoga of equal vision Krishna has described could stay stable, because the mind is so restless. He voices an honest doubt shared by many practitioners.",
  '6.34': "Arjuna explains his concern: the mind is fickle, turbulent, powerful and stubborn, and restraining it seems as hard as restraining the wind. His candid admission makes the difficulty of meditation real.",
  '6.36': "Krishna adds that yoga is hard to reach for someone without self-control, yet a person who masters the mind and strives with the right method can attain it. Effort and proper means make success possible.",
  '6.37': "Arjuna asks what becomes of a person who begins the path with faith but lacks perseverance, whose mind slips away from yoga before reaching perfection. His question concerns the fate of the sincere but unsuccessful seeker.",
  '6.38': "Arjuna worries that such a seeker, confused on the path to the Absolute and left without footing, might vanish like a torn cloud, losing both worldly success and spiritual attainment.",
  '6.39': "Arjuna asks Krishna to remove this doubt completely, saying no one besides Krishna is able to resolve it. His appeal shows growing trust in Krishna as the final source of clarity.",
  '6.40': "Krishna reassures Arjuna that such a seeker meets no ruin in this world or the next, since one who does good never comes to a bad end. Sincere spiritual effort is never wasted.",
  '6.41': "Krishna explains that the unsuccessful yogi spends long ages in heavenly realms earned by the pious and is then born into a virtuous, prosperous family, where practice can begin again.",
  '6.42': "Alternatively, such a seeker may be born into a family of wise yogis, a birth Krishna calls even rarer in this world. Such surroundings give the best opportunity to continue spiritual growth.",
  '6.43': "In that new birth, the seeker regains the spiritual understanding developed in the previous life and strives again, even harder, for perfection. Progress on the path carries forward across lifetimes.",
  '6.44': "By the force of past practice, such a person is drawn toward yoga almost involuntarily, and even someone merely curious about yoga rises beyond the ritual portions of scripture. Spiritual impressions endure.",
  '6.45': "When the yogi strives earnestly and is cleansed of all impurity, the perfection built over many lifetimes carries them to the supreme destination. Patient, sincere effort culminates in the highest goal.",
  '6.46': "Krishna declares the yogi superior to ascetics, to those devoted to learning and to performers of ritual work, and urges Arjuna to become a yogi. Linking with the Divine surpasses these partial approaches.",
  '6.47': "Concluding the chapter, Krishna says that among all yogis, the one who worships him with faith and an inner self absorbed in him is the most closely united with him. *Bhakti* is revealed as the summit of yoga.",
  '7.1': "Krishna begins a new teaching, telling Arjuna that by keeping the mind attached to him and practicing yoga under his shelter, he will come to know Krishna fully and without doubt.",
  '7.2': "Krishna promises to explain both knowledge and its practical realization in full, after which nothing further in this world would remain to be known. The verse sets up the chapter's revelation.",
  '7.3': "Krishna observes that very few people seek spiritual perfection, and even among those who attain it, rare is the one who understands him as he truly is. Knowing Krishna in truth is extremely uncommon.",
  '7.4': "Krishna lists the eight components of his separated, lower energy: earth, water, fire, air, space, mind, intelligence and ego. Both the physical and subtle worlds are presented as his energy, distinct from him.",
  '7.5': "Beyond this lower nature, Krishna describes a higher energy made up of the living beings, the souls who sustain the world. Living entities are shown to be his superior energy, spiritual in nature.",
  '7.6': "Krishna explains that all beings arise from these two energies, and that he himself is the origin and the dissolution of the entire universe. He stands as the ultimate source behind everything.",
  '7.8': "Krishna begins showing how he can be sensed in ordinary experience, naming himself as the essence of things such as water's taste, the radiance of sun and moon, the syllable om in scripture and human capability.",
  '7.9': "Continuing, Krishna identifies himself as the essential quality in things: earth's pure scent, fire's heat, the vitality of every creature and the self-discipline of ascetics. Everyday perception can become remembrance of him.",
  '7.10': "Krishna calls himself the eternal seed from which all beings spring, and the source of intelligence in the wise and brilliance in the powerful. Every living thing and every capacity traces back to him.",
  '7.11': "Krishna says he is the strength of the strong when free of lust and attachment, and the desire within beings that stays in harmony with *dharma*. Desire aligned with righteousness becomes a manifestation of him.",
  '7.12': "Krishna explains that every state of being in goodness, passion or ignorance comes from him alone. These qualities exist within his energy while he himself remains free of their influence.",
  '7.13': "Bewildered by the three modes of nature, the whole world fails to recognize Krishna, who stands above them and never diminishes. The modes cloud perception and hide the Supreme from view.",
  '7.15': "Krishna describes those who refuse to surrender to him, such as the foolish and the wicked. Their knowledge has been stolen by illusion, and they cling to a demonic mindset that keeps them far from him.",
  '7.16': "Krishna says four kinds of pious people turn to him in devotion: the distressed, the curious, those seeking wealth and the wise. He acknowledges that various motives can bring people to him.",
  '7.17': "Among these four, Krishna says the wise one, always engaged and devoted to him alone, stands out. Krishna holds such a person exceedingly dear, and that person holds Krishna equally dear.",
  '7.18': "Krishna calls all four generous souls, yet he regards the wise devotee as his very own self, since that person is fixed on him as the supreme goal. Pure devotion brings the deepest intimacy.",
  '7.19': "Krishna explains that after many lifetimes the truly wise person surrenders to him, realizing that Vasudeva is everything. Such a great soul, a *mahatma*, is extremely rare. The verse marks the fulfillment of long spiritual searching.",
  '7.20': "Krishna explains that people whose understanding is carried away by various desires approach other gods, following particular rules according to their own nature. Material wants shape the way people worship.",
  '7.21': "Krishna explains that when a worshipper wishes to honor a particular deity with faith, he himself makes that faith steady. Even worship of other gods depends on Krishna's sanction.",
  '7.22': "Endowed with that faith, the worshipper seeks the favor of that deity and receives the desired benefits, yet those benefits are actually arranged by Krishna alone. He is the hidden source behind every blessing.",
  '7.23': "Krishna notes that the results gained by people of limited understanding are temporary. Worshippers of the gods go to the gods, while his devotees come to him. Devotion leads to an eternal destination.",
  '7.24': "Krishna says unintelligent people imagine that he was formless and has merely taken on a visible form, failing to understand his supreme, imperishable and unsurpassed nature. His personal form is eternal.",
  '7.25': "Krishna explains that he is not revealed to everyone, being veiled by his internal power, *yoga-maya*. The bewildered world therefore does not recognize him as unborn and unchanging.",
  '7.26': "Krishna declares that he knows all beings of the past, present and future, yet no one truly knows him. His knowledge is complete, while everyone else's understanding of him remains limited.",
  '7.27': "Krishna explains that all living beings enter creation in delusion, bewildered by the dualities that arise from desire and aversion. This basic confusion keeps souls bound and blinds them to the truth.",
  '7.28': "People whose sins have come to an end through virtuous deeds become free from the delusion of dualities and worship Krishna with firm resolve. Purification allows steady devotion to emerge.",
  '7.29': "Those who take shelter of Krishna and strive for freedom from old age and death come to know Brahman, the self and the whole of action. Devotion brings complete spiritual understanding.",
  '7.30': "Krishna promises that people who understand him as the ruler behind matter, the demigods and every sacrifice, and who keep their minds fixed on him, will recognize him even when death arrives.",
  '8.1': "Opening a new chapter, Arjuna puts a string of questions to Krishna, wanting the exact meaning of Brahman, the self and karma, and of the principles that rule matter and the demigods. He wants the terms just introduced defined.",
  '8.2': "Arjuna further asks who the lord of sacrifice within the body is and how he dwells there, and how self-controlled people can know Krishna at the time of death. His questions set the chapter's theme.",
  '8.3': "Krishna answers that Brahman is the imperishable Supreme, the self is the soul's essential nature, and action is the creative force that brings beings into existence. He begins defining Arjuna's terms.",
  '8.4': "Krishna continues: the material plane is perishable nature, the cosmic person is the basis of the gods, and he himself, present within every body as Supersoul, is the lord of sacrifice.",
  '8.6': "Krishna teaches that the thoughts filling one's mind at the moment of death determine the next destination, and those final thoughts are shaped by what one dwelt on throughout life.",
  '8.7': "Krishna therefore tells Arjuna to remember him at all times while still doing his duty to fight. With mind and intellect offered to Krishna, Arjuna is certain to reach him. Devotion and duty work together.",
  '8.8': "One who meditates on the Supreme Person with a mind trained by practice and never straying elsewhere reaches that divine Person. Krishna emphasizes the power of constant, focused remembrance.",
  '8.9': "Krishna describes the one to meditate on: all-knowing, ancient, the ruler and sustainer of everything, subtler than the atom, inconceivable in form, radiant like the sun and beyond all darkness.",
  '8.10': "At death, one who fixes the life breath between the eyebrows by yogic strength, with an unwavering mind and full devotion, reaches the supreme divine Person. Devotion is the heart of this practice.",
  '8.11': "Krishna introduces the imperishable goal that Vedic scholars describe, that detached renunciants enter and for which seekers practice celibacy. He promises to explain this path briefly.",
  '8.12': "Krishna describes yogic departure: closing all the gates of the senses, confining the mind in the heart and drawing the life breath up to the head, one becomes established in yogic concentration.",
  '8.13': "Steady in that practice, one who utters the single sacred syllable om while remembering Krishna and then leaves the body reaches the supreme destination. Remembrance of Krishna at death is decisive.",
  '8.14': "Krishna says he is easily reached by the yogi who remembers him constantly and exclusively, always engaged in that remembrance. Unbroken devotion makes the Supreme accessible.",
  '8.15': "Krishna assures Arjuna that great souls who come to him have reached life's highest perfection. They never again return to this passing world of misery, because attaining him ends the cycle of birth.",
  '8.16': "Krishna explains that every world in the material universe, even Brahma's highest realm, involves repeated birth, while one who reaches him is never born again. Only his abode offers permanent freedom.",
  '8.17': "Krishna describes cosmic time: a single day of Brahma spans a thousand ages, and his night is equally long. Those who understand this grasp the vast scale and impermanence of the universe.",
  '8.18': "When Brahma's day begins, all beings emerge from the unmanifest; when his night falls, they merge back into that unmanifest state. The universe moves in vast cycles of appearance and dissolution.",
  '8.19': "Krishna explains that the same multitude of beings comes into existence again and again and helplessly dissolves when Brahma's night arrives, appearing once more at his day. Material existence is endlessly cyclical.",
  '8.20': "Krishna reveals another unmanifest nature, eternal and beyond this cosmic cycle, which is never destroyed even when all beings perish. A spiritual reality lies beyond material dissolution.",
  '8.21': "This eternal unmanifest is called imperishable and the supreme destination. Krishna identifies it as his own supreme abode, from which no one returns once they reach it.",
  '8.22': "Krishna explains that the Supreme Person, within whom all beings exist and who pervades everything, is attained by undivided devotion. *Bhakti* is presented as the direct means of reaching him.",
  '8.23': "Krishna introduces the times at which yogis depart this world, explaining which lead to no return and which lead to rebirth. He sets up a description of two paths after death.",
  '8.24': "Krishna describes the path of light: knowers of Brahman who depart under the influence of fire, light, day, the bright lunar fortnight and the sun's northern course go to Brahman.",
  '8.25': "Krishna describes the path of darkness: a yogi departing under smoke, night, the dark lunar fortnight and the sun's southern course reaches the lunar realm and then returns to birth.",
  '8.26': "Krishna calls these two paths, the bright and the dark, perennial routes out of this world. One leads to no return, while the other brings the soul back again.",
  '8.27': "Krishna explains that a yogi who knows these paths is never bewildered, and he urges Arjuna to remain fixed in yoga at all times. A devotee absorbed in Krishna need not worry about the timing of death.",
  '8.28': "Krishna concludes that the yogi who understands this teaching goes beyond the merit earned through Vedic study, sacrifice, austerity and charity, and reaches the supreme, original abode.",
  '9.1': "Krishna announces that he will now reveal the most confidential knowledge, together with its realization, to Arjuna because he is free from envy. Knowing it releases one from all misfortune.",
  '9.2': "Krishna calls this knowledge the king of sciences and the greatest secret, supremely purifying. It can be directly experienced, accords with *dharma*, is joyful to practice and never perishes.",
  '9.3': "Krishna warns that those without faith in this path fail to reach him and return to the road of repeated birth and death. Faith is the essential qualification for progress.",
  '9.4': "Krishna explains that he pervades the entire universe in his unmanifest form. All beings rest in him, yet he does not rest in them. He is both present everywhere and beyond everything.",
  '9.5': "Krishna adds a paradox: in another sense beings do not rest in him either, and he invites Arjuna to see his mystic power. He sustains all beings while his own self remains beyond them.",
  '9.6': "Krishna offers an analogy: just as the mighty wind blows everywhere yet always remains within space, all beings remain within him. The image shows how he holds creation without being diminished.",
  '9.7': "Krishna explains that at the end of a cosmic cycle all beings enter his material nature, and at the start of the next cycle he sends them forth again. Creation and dissolution happen under his control.",
  '9.8': "Directing his own material nature, Krishna repeatedly brings forth the whole multitude of beings, who are helplessly subject to nature's power. He stands as the master of the cosmic cycles.",
  '9.9': "Krishna says these acts of creation do not bind him, since he remains detached, seated as if neutral. His example shows how action without attachment brings no bondage.",
  '9.10': "Krishna explains that material nature produces all moving and unmoving beings under his supervision, and this is why the world keeps turning. Nature functions only under his direction.",
  '9.11': "Krishna says fools disregard him when he appears in a human form, unaware of his supreme nature as the great Lord of all beings. His humanlike form is fully divine.",
  '9.12': "Those who take on deluding demonic and godless natures have empty hopes, fruitless actions and futile knowledge. Krishna describes how turning from the Divine leaves life bewildered and unfulfilled.",
  '9.13': "In contrast, great souls take shelter of the divine nature and worship Krishna with undivided minds, knowing him as the imperishable origin of all beings. Their devotion grows from real understanding.",
  '9.14': "Krishna describes how great souls worship him: always singing his glories, striving with firm vows and bowing to him in devotion, ever united with him. The verse portrays devotional life in action.",
  '9.15': "Krishna notes that others worship him through the sacrifice of knowledge, seeing him as one, as distinct, or as the universal form facing every direction. These varied approaches still center on him.",
  '9.16': "Krishna declares that every part of Vedic worship is himself: the rite, the sacrifice, the offering to ancestors, the healing herb, the mantra, the ghee, the fire and the act of offering.",
  '9.17': "Krishna says he is the source and support of the universe, its mother, father and grandsire alike. The syllable om, the Vedas and all that purifies or deserves to be known also point back to him.",
  '9.18': "Krishna describes himself as the goal, sustainer, master, witness, home, refuge and dearest friend of all, as well as their origin, end, foundation and imperishable seed. He is the complete shelter for every soul.",
  '9.19': "Krishna says he gives heat, withholds and releases the rain, and is both immortality and death, what exists and what does not. All opposites in nature ultimately depend on him.",
  '9.20': "Krishna describes followers of the three Vedas who drink soma and worship him indirectly through sacrifice, praying for heaven. Cleansed of sin, they reach Indra's realm and enjoy celestial pleasures.",
  '9.21': "After enjoying heaven's vast pleasures, they return to the mortal world once their merit runs out. Following Vedic ritual for the sake of desire, they keep coming and going. Heavenly rewards are temporary.",
  '9.23': "Krishna says that even those who faithfully worship other gods are actually worshipping him, though in an improper way. Their faith is genuine, yet their approach misses the direct path.",
  '9.24': "Krishna reveals that every sacrifice is ultimately meant for him, the true enjoyer and Lord. People who do not understand this in truth slip back into repeated birth, so recognizing him gives worship its real purpose.",
  '9.25': "Krishna explains that worshippers of the gods go to the gods, of ancestors to the ancestors, of spirits to the spirits, and his own worshippers come to him. Worship determines one's destination.",
  '9.26': "Krishna reveals that he accepts even the humblest gift, such as a single leaf or a little water, when a devotee offers it with love. What pleases him is the devotion behind the offering, not its worth.",
  '9.28': "Krishna promises that by offering everything to him, Arjuna will be freed from the bondage of good and bad results of action. With a mind joined in renunciation and yoga, he will be liberated and reach Krishna.",
  '9.29': "Krishna says he is equal toward all beings, hating none and favoring none, yet those who worship him with devotion live in him, and he lives in them. Love creates a special, reciprocal bond.",
  '9.30': "Krishna declares that even someone with very bad conduct should be regarded as saintly if they worship him with undivided devotion, because their resolve is rightly placed. Devotion outweighs past faults.",
  '9.31': "Such a person quickly becomes righteous and finds lasting peace. Krishna tells Arjuna to announce firmly that a devotee of Krishna will never be ruined. The verse assures protection for those devoted to him.",
  '9.32': "Krishna states that anyone who takes refuge in him, whatever their birth, including women, merchants and laborers, can reach the supreme destination. Devotion is open to all without restriction.",
  '9.33': "Krishna reasons that if this is true for all, it is even more certain for pious brahmanas and saintly kings who are devoted. Having come into this temporary, joyless world, Arjuna should worship him.",
  '9.34': "Krishna ends the chapter by asking Arjuna to fill his mind with him, become his devotee, worship him and bow to him. Making Krishna his whole aim in this way, Arjuna will reach him.",
  '10.1': "Krishna tells Arjuna he will speak further supreme words, offered out of a wish for Arjuna's welfare because Arjuna delights in hearing him. Chapter ten's teaching on Krishna's glories opens as an act of affection.",
  '10.2': "Krishna states that neither the gods nor the great sages know his origin, since he himself is the source of all of them. His position precedes even the highest beings, so knowing him depends on his own revelation.",
  '10.3': "Krishna teaches that one who knows him as unborn, beginningless and the great Lord of all worlds is free from delusion and released from all sins. Right understanding of who Krishna is becomes a liberating force.",
  '10.4': "Beginning a list completed in the next verse, Krishna names inner states such as intelligence, knowledge, clarity, forgiveness, truthfulness, self-control, calm, pleasure and pain, birth and death, fear and fearlessness, all of which come from him.",
  '10.5': "Krishna completes the list with nonviolence, equanimity, contentment, austerity, charity, fame and infamy, declaring that all these varied states of living beings arise from him alone. Even the qualities of the mind trace back to one source.",
  '10.6': "Krishna explains that the seven great sages, the four earlier sages and the Manus were born from his mind and share his nature, and from them descend all the creatures of the worlds. Every line of creation leads back to him.",
  '10.7': "Krishna says that whoever truly understands his opulence (*vibhuti*) and his mystic power becomes joined to him in unwavering yoga, beyond any doubt. Knowing Krishna's glories steadies the heart in devotion.",
  '10.8': "In one of the chapter's four core verses, Krishna declares himself the source of everything, from whom all things proceed. The wise who grasp this worship him with deep feeling, showing that knowing Krishna naturally flowers into *bhakti*.",
  '10.9': "Krishna describes his devotees: their minds and lives are absorbed in him, they enlighten one another and speak of him constantly, and they find satisfaction and joy in this. Devotion appears as a shared, joyful conversation centred on Krishna.",
  '10.10': "To those always engaged in loving devotion, Krishna gives the intelligence (*buddhi-yoga*) by which they come to him. Progress on the devotional path is guided by Krishna's own gift of understanding.",
  '10.11': "Krishna explains that, moved by kindness toward his devotees, he lights a radiant lamp of wisdom from within their hearts. That light drives away the gloom that ignorance produces, a direct gift of his mercy.",
  '10.12': "Arjuna responds by acknowledging Krishna as the Supreme Brahman, the supreme abode and the purest, the eternal divine Person, the original God, unborn and all-pervading. Having heard the core verses, Arjuna affirms Krishna's position in his own words.",
  '10.13': "Arjuna notes that great sages such as Narada, Asita, Devala and Vyasa have all proclaimed Krishna's supremacy, and now Krishna confirms it himself. Arjuna's acceptance rests on both revered tradition and Krishna's own words.",
  '10.14': "Arjuna accepts everything Krishna has said as true, adding that neither the gods nor the demons understand Krishna's personality. The verse models faithful acceptance of revelation where ordinary powers of knowing fall short.",
  '10.15': "Arjuna praises Krishna with exalted titles, calling him the source and ruler of every being and lord of the cosmos. He confesses that Krishna alone truly knows himself, beyond the reach of anyone else's understanding.",
  '10.16': "Arjuna asks Krishna to describe in full his divine opulences, by which he pervades and abides in all these worlds. The request sets up the long catalogue of glories that follows.",
  '10.17': "Arjuna asks how he can come to know Krishna through constant meditation, and in which particular forms or things he should think of him. He seeks practical ways to remember Krishna throughout the world.",
  '10.18': "Arjuna asks Krishna to describe his yoga and opulences again in detail, saying he never tires of hearing such nectar. The verse captures the devotee's endless taste for hearing about Krishna.",
  '10.19': "Krishna agrees to describe his divine opulences, though only the most prominent ones, since there is no end to their extent. The listing that follows is a sample of a limitless reality.",
  '10.21': "Krishna names himself as Vishnu among the Adityas, the radiant sun among lights, Marichi among the Maruts, and the moon among the stars. Each case shows Krishna as the excellence that stands out within a group.",
  '10.22': "Krishna says he is the Sama Veda among the Vedas, Indra among the gods, the mind among the senses, and consciousness in living beings. The list moves from scripture and heaven to the inner life of every creature.",
  '10.23': "Krishna identifies himself as Shankara among the Rudras, Kuvera among the Yakshas and Rakshasas, fire among the Vasus, and Meru among mountains. Powerful deities and great landmarks alike display his splendour.",
  '10.24': "Krishna names himself as Brihaspati, chief of priests, Skanda among military commanders, and the ocean among bodies of water. Leadership, valour and vastness each point toward him.",
  '10.25': "Krishna says he is Bhrigu among the great sages, the single syllable Om among sounds, the sacrifice of chanting (*japa*) among sacrifices, and the Himalayas among immovable things. The mention of japa highlights the special place of repeating sacred names.",
  '10.26': "Krishna names himself as the sacred fig tree among trees, Narada among divine sages, Chitraratha among the Gandharvas, and the sage Kapila among perfected beings. Nature, devotion, art and wisdom all reflect him.",
  '10.27': "Continuing his list of glories, Krishna names the finest among animals and people, such as the nectar-born horse Uchchaihshrava and the human monarch. Whatever stands out as supreme in any class reflects his power.",
  '10.28': "Krishna identifies himself as the thunderbolt among weapons, the wish-fulfilling cow among cows, Kandarpa as the force of procreation, and Vasuki among serpents. Even the power to generate life is presented as his.",
  '10.29': "Krishna names himself as Ananta among the Nagas, Varuna among water beings, Aryama among the ancestors, and Yama among those who enforce discipline. Each represents the foremost of its kind.",
  '10.30': "Krishna says he is Prahlada among the Daityas, time among those that count, the lion among beasts, and Garuda among birds. Prahlada, a devotee born in a demon family, shows that devotion can shine anywhere.",
  '10.31': "Krishna identifies himself as the wind among purifiers, Rama among wielders of weapons, the makara among sea creatures, and the Ganges among rivers. Purity, heroism and sanctity are presented as his reflections.",
  '10.32': "Krishna says he is the beginning, middle and end of all creations, the knowledge of the self among sciences, and the decisive conclusion in debate. He is both the frame of existence and the highest aim of learning.",
  '10.33': "Krishna names himself as the letter A among letters, the dual compound among word forms, inexhaustible time, and the creator whose faces turn everywhere. From language to cosmic time, he stands at the root of things.",
  '10.34': "Krishna identifies himself both with death, which takes everything away, and with the birth of all that will come. He also names noble feminine virtues, such as memory and patience, as expressions of his presence.",
  '10.35': "Krishna identifies himself as the Brihat Sama among hymns, the Gayatri among meters, Margashirsha among months, and flower-filled spring among seasons. Beauty in sacred sound and in nature both reveal him.",
  '10.36': "Krishna says he is gambling among cheats, the splendour of the splendid, victory, determination, and the strength of the strong. Even the leading instance of a dubious activity draws its power from him, as all excellence does.",
  '10.37': "Krishna names himself as Vasudeva among the Vrishnis, Arjuna among the Pandavas, Vyasa among sages, and Ushana among thinkers. By including himself and Arjuna, he shows his glory within the very scene of the dialogue.",
  '10.38': "Krishna says he is the rod of punishment among those who restrain, wise policy among those seeking victory, silence among secrets, and the wisdom of the wise. Order, strategy and knowledge each carry his presence.",
  '10.39': "Krishna declares that he is the seed of all beings, and nothing moving or unmoving can exist without him. This sums up the catalogue: every particular glory rests on his being the origin of all.",
  '10.40': "Krishna tells Arjuna that his divine opulences are endless, and what he has described is only a brief indication of their vastness. The whole catalogue is meant to point beyond itself.",
  '10.41': "Krishna teaches that whatever is glorious, beautiful or powerful should be understood as arising from a mere spark of his splendour. This gives a simple rule for seeing him in any excellence in the world.",
  '10.42': "Krishna concludes that detailed knowledge is unnecessary, since he supports the entire universe with a single portion of himself. The whole cosmos rests on a fraction of Krishna, hinting at his far greater transcendent being.",
  '11.1': "Arjuna says that Krishna's confidential teaching about the self, given out of kindness, has dispelled his illusion. Chapter eleven opens with Arjuna's gratitude as he prepares to ask for something more.",
  '11.2': "Arjuna says he has heard from Krishna in detail about the arising and passing away of beings, as well as Krishna's own imperishable greatness. Calling Krishna lotus-eyed, he mixes reverence with affection.",
  '11.3': "Arjuna accepts Krishna's account of himself as true, yet wishes to see his majestic, sovereign form. Addressing Krishna as Supreme Lord and Supreme Person, he seeks to witness directly what he has heard.",
  '11.4': "Arjuna humbly asks Krishna, master of yoga, to reveal his imperishable self if Krishna thinks Arjuna is able to see it. The request leaves the decision entirely to Krishna.",
  '11.5': "Krishna invites Arjuna to behold his hundreds and thousands of divine forms, varied in colour and shape. The revelation of the universal form begins with a call to look.",
  '11.6': "Krishna tells Arjuna to see the Adityas, Vasus, Rudras, Ashvins and Maruts, along with many wonders never seen before. The gods themselves appear as parts of Krishna's cosmic form.",
  '11.7': "Krishna invites Arjuna to see the entire universe, moving and unmoving, gathered in one place within his body, along with anything else he wishes to see. The whole cosmos is shown resting within Krishna.",
  '11.8': "Krishna explains that Arjuna cannot see this form with ordinary eyes, so he grants him divine sight to behold his mystic majesty. Seeing God depends on God's own gift.",
  '11.9': "Sanjaya tells King Dhritarashtra that Hari, the great master of yoga, then revealed his supreme majestic form to Arjuna. The narrator marks the moment the vision actually unfolds.",
  '11.10': "Sanjaya describes the form as having countless mouths and eyes, many wondrous sights, numerous divine ornaments, and many raised divine weapons. The verse conveys the overwhelming multiplicity of the universal form.",
  '11.11': "Sanjaya continues that the form wears divine garlands and garments, is anointed with heavenly fragrances, and is full of wonder, limitless, with faces in every direction. The description stresses both beauty and infinity.",
  '11.12': "Sanjaya says that if a thousand suns rose at once in the sky, their light might resemble the radiance of that great being. The image conveys a brilliance beyond ordinary comparison.",
  '11.13': "Sanjaya says Arjuna then saw the whole universe, divided in countless ways, standing together in the body of the God of gods. Multiplicity and unity appear at once within Krishna.",
  '11.14': "Struck with amazement and trembling with emotion, Arjuna lowers his head before Krishna and folds his hands to speak. His astonishment immediately becomes humble reverence.",
  '11.15': "Gazing at the universal form, Arjuna describes seeing every god and countless kinds of beings inside Krishna's body, including Brahma on his lotus seat. All levels of creation appear within that single form.",
  '11.16': "Arjuna sees countless arms, bellies, mouths and eyes spreading in every direction, and finds no end, middle or beginning to the form. He addresses Krishna as Lord of the universe whose form is the universe.",
  '11.17': "Arjuna sees Krishna with crown, club and disc, a mass of radiance blazing everywhere, hard to look at, shining like fire and sun, and immeasurable. Familiar emblems of Vishnu appear within the dazzling vision.",
  '11.18': "Arjuna declares Krishna the imperishable supreme truth to be known, the final resting place of the universe, the unchanging protector of eternal dharma, and the timeless Person. Vision becomes theological recognition.",
  '11.19': "Arjuna sees Krishna without beginning, middle or end, of infinite power and countless arms, with sun and moon as eyes and a mouth of blazing fire, heating the whole universe with his own radiance.",
  '11.20': "Arjuna sees that Krishna alone fills the space between heaven and earth and every direction, and that the three worlds tremble at this wondrous, fierce form. The vision begins to turn frightening.",
  '11.21': "Arjuna sees hosts of gods entering Krishna, some praying in fear with joined palms, while great sages and perfected beings wish for well-being and praise him with abundant hymns. All beings respond with awe and worship.",
  '11.22': "Arjuna names the Rudras, Adityas, Vasus, Sadhyas, Vishvadevas, Ashvins, Maruts, ancestors, Gandharvas, Yakshas, Asuras and Siddhas, all gazing at Krishna in amazement. Even celestial beings are astonished by the form.",
  '11.23': "Arjuna says that seeing the vast form with many mouths, eyes, arms, thighs, feet, bellies and terrible teeth, the worlds are shaken, and so is he. Wonder gives way to fear.",
  '11.24': "Seeing Krishna touching the sky, blazing in many colours, with gaping mouths and huge fiery eyes, Arjuna admits his heart trembles and he can find neither steadiness nor peace. He addresses Krishna as Vishnu.",
  '11.25': "Seeing the fearsome tusked mouths blazing like the fire of cosmic dissolution, Arjuna loses all sense of direction and finds no comfort. He pleads for Krishna's grace as Lord of gods and refuge of the worlds.",
  '11.26': "Arjuna sees Dhritarashtra's sons and their allied kings, along with Bhishma, Drona, Karna and leading warriors of his own side, rushing toward Krishna's mouths. The coming battle's outcome is revealed within the vision.",
  '11.27': "Arjuna sees the warriors hurrying into Krishna's terrifying, tusked mouths, some caught between the teeth with their heads crushed. The vision shows their deaths already sealed by time.",
  '11.28': "Arjuna compares the heroes of the human world entering Krishna's flaming mouths to many rivers rushing toward the ocean. The image conveys the inevitability with which all beings flow toward their end.",
  '11.29': "Arjuna compares the worlds rushing into Krishna's mouths to moths flying swiftly into a blazing fire to their destruction. The image stresses the speed and helplessness of beings drawn toward death.",
  '11.30': "Arjuna sees Krishna licking up all the worlds from every side with flaming mouths, filling the universe with fierce rays that scorch it. Krishna here appears as all-consuming time.",
  '11.31': "Terrified, Arjuna bows and asks Krishna who he is in this fierce form, begging for his grace and saying he wants to understand his purpose. Arjuna seeks the meaning behind the vision.",
  '11.32': "Krishna answers that he is time (*kala*), the mighty destroyer of worlds, now engaged in ending them. Even without Arjuna's part, the warriors in the opposing ranks will not survive; the outcome rests in Krishna's hands.",
  '11.33': "Krishna urges Arjuna to rise, win glory, conquer his foes and enjoy a prosperous kingdom, since Krishna has already slain these warriors. Arjuna is to be merely the instrument, acting in harmony with Krishna's will.",
  '11.34': "Krishna names Drona, Bhishma, Jayadratha, Karna and other heroes as already slain by him, and tells Arjuna to fight without distress, assuring him of victory. Arjuna's fear for the outcome is answered directly.",
  '11.35': "Sanjaya relates that after hearing Krishna's words, Arjuna, trembling and with joined palms, bowed again and again and spoke to Krishna in a choked voice, filled with fear. Awe and humility now overwhelm the warrior.",
  '11.36': "Arjuna says it is fitting that the world rejoices and is drawn to Krishna by hearing his glories, while demonic beings flee in fear and perfected beings bow to him. He addresses Krishna as Hrishikesha, master of the senses.",
  '11.37': "Arjuna asks why beings would not bow to Krishna, who is greater even than Brahma, the first creator, infinite, Lord of gods and refuge of the universe, both the manifest and unmanifest and what lies beyond them.",
  '11.38': "Arjuna praises Krishna as the original God and ancient Person, the supreme resting place of the universe, the knower and the known, and the highest abode, by whom the whole cosmos is pervaded.",
  '11.39': "Arjuna identifies Krishna with Vayu, Yama, Agni, Varuna, the moon, Prajapati and the great-grandsire, and offers him obeisances a thousand times, again and again. Recognition of Krishna's supremacy pours out as repeated homage.",
  '11.40': "Overcome with awe, Arjuna bows to Krishna again and again in every direction and praises his limitless strength. Seeing that Krishna pervades all things, he recognizes him as present in everything.",
  '11.41': "Arjuna recalls that, thinking of Krishna as a friend, he casually called him Krishna, Yadava and friend, unaware of his greatness, whether from carelessness or affection. He begins to seek forgiveness.",
  '11.42': "Arjuna asks forgiveness for any disrespect shown to Krishna in jest, while playing, resting, sitting or eating, whether alone or in company. The verse reveals the intimacy of their friendship alongside Arjuna's new awe.",
  '11.43': "Arjuna praises Krishna as father of the moving and unmoving world, its worshipable and greatest teacher, with no equal in the three worlds, let alone anyone greater. His power is beyond compare.",
  '11.44': "Arjuna prostrates himself and asks the worshipable Lord to be gracious and bear with him as a father does a son, a friend a friend, or a lover the beloved. These images point to the intimate relationships of devotion.",
  '11.45': "Arjuna confesses he is thrilled to see what was never seen before, yet his mind is shaken with fear, and he asks Krishna to show again his familiar form. Awe alone cannot satisfy a heart that longs for intimacy.",
  '11.46': "Arjuna asks to see Krishna again in the four-armed form, crowned and holding club and disc, asking the thousand-armed universal form to take that shape. He longs to move from the cosmic to the personal.",
  '11.47': "Krishna tells Arjuna that, pleased with him, he revealed this supreme, radiant, infinite and original universal form by his own power, a form no one else had seen before. The vision was an act of Krishna's grace.",
  '11.48': "Krishna says that no one but Arjuna in the human world can see this form through Vedic study, sacrifice, charity, rituals or severe austerities. Such vision comes by Krishna's favour alone.",
  '11.49': "Krishna reassures Arjuna, telling him to be free from distress and confusion at having seen this terrible form, and to look again, fearless and glad at heart, upon his familiar form.",
  '11.50': "Sanjaya relates that Krishna, having spoken, showed his own form once more and then, taking his gentle human-like form, comforted the frightened Arjuna. The fearsome vision gives way to Krishna's beauty.",
  '11.51': "Seeing Krishna's gentle human-like form again, Arjuna says he is now composed and restored to his natural state. Peace returns when Krishna appears in his intimate, personal form.",
  '11.52': "Krishna tells Arjuna that the form he is now seeing is extremely rare to behold, and even the gods constantly long for a sight of it. In the devotional reading this refers to Krishna's own human-like form.",
  '11.53': "Krishna states that he cannot be seen as Arjuna has seen him through study of the Vedas, austerity, charity or worship. Ordinary spiritual practices alone do not grant such vision; the following verse names what does.",
  '11.55': "Krishna declares that one who works for him, holds him as the highest goal, is devoted to him, free from attachment and without enmity toward any being comes to him. The verse is often seen as the essence of the Gita.",
  '12.1': "Arjuna asks who is more perfect in yoga: devotees who always worship Krishna with steady devotion, or those who worship the imperishable, unmanifest Absolute. Chapter twelve opens by comparing personal and impersonal worship.",
  '12.2': "Answering Arjuna's question, Krishna says the most perfect yogis are those who fix their thoughts on him and serve him without pause with deep faith. He places loving devotion to his personal form above every other path.",
  '12.3': "Krishna turns to those who worship the imperishable, indescribable, unmanifest Absolute, which is all-pervading, inconceivable, unchanging and fixed. The verse describes the object of impersonal meditation, and the thought continues in the next verse.",
  '12.4': "Krishna says that those who restrain their senses, keep an even mind toward everything and work for the welfare of all beings also reach him. Even the impersonal path ultimately leads to Krishna.",
  '12.5': "Krishna admits that meditating on the formless absolute is a harder road. Embodied beings struggle to fix the mind on something with no form, which is why loving service to the personal Lord comes more easily.",
  '12.6': "Krishna describes devotees who offer all their actions to him, take him as their supreme goal, and worship him with undivided devotion, meditating on him. This sets up the promise of the next verse.",
  '12.7': "Krishna promises Arjuna that he quickly rescues devotees whose thoughts rest on him, lifting them out of the vast sea of repeated death. Their freedom comes through his personal help, not their own effort alone.",
  '12.9': "Krishna says that if Arjuna cannot fix his mind steadily on him, he should seek to reach him through regular disciplined practice (*abhyasa-yoga*). Krishna begins offering a ladder of steps suited to different capacities.",
  '12.10': "If even regular practice is beyond him, Krishna tells Arjuna to devote himself to work done for Krishna's sake, since acting for him also brings perfection. Ordinary action becomes a path of devotional service.",
  '12.11': "If even that is not possible, Krishna advises Arjuna to take shelter in him and, with self-control, give up the fruits of all his actions. Renouncing results is the most accessible starting step on the ladder.",
  '12.12': "Krishna ranks the steps: knowledge is better than mere practice, meditation better than knowledge, and giving up the fruits of action better still, since peace follows such renunciation at once. The verse explains why releasing results matters.",
  '12.14': "Krishna describes the devotee dear to him as always content, self-controlled, firm in resolve, with mind and intelligence offered to him. This continues the portrait of the devotee begun in the previous verse.",
  '12.15': "Krishna says the devotee who neither disturbs the world nor is disturbed by it, and who is free from elation, anger, fear and anxiety, is dear to him. Inner peace and harmlessness go together.",
  '12.16': "Krishna calls dear the devotee who is free from dependence, pure, capable, unconcerned, untroubled, and who gives up self-centred undertakings. Such qualities mark a life resting wholly on Krishna.",
  '12.17': "Krishna declares dear the devotee who neither rejoices nor hates, neither grieves nor craves, and who has given up attachment to both auspicious and inauspicious outcomes. Devotion steadies the heart beyond worldly ups and downs.",
  '12.18': "Krishna describes the devotee as equal toward enemy and friend, in honour and dishonour, in cold and heat, pleasure and pain, and free from attachment. The description continues into the next verse.",
  '12.19': "Krishna says the devotee who is even in blame and praise, restrained in speech, content with whatever comes, unattached to a home and steady in mind is dear to him. The portrait of the ideal devotee concludes.",
  '12.20': "Krishna says that those who follow this immortal teaching of dharma with faith, holding him as the supreme goal, are exceedingly dear to him. The chapter closes by showing the devotee's love returned by Krishna.",
  '13.1': "Arjuna opens a new line of inquiry, asking Krishna to explain matter and spirit, the field and its knower, and what true knowledge is along with the object it seeks to know. These questions frame the whole chapter.",
  '13.2': "Krishna introduces a key distinction: the body serves as the field where life is lived and experienced, while the conscious self that is aware of that body is what the wise term the field's knower.",
  '13.3': "Krishna reveals that he too is a knower present in every body, as the Supersoul alongside each individual self. Understanding the body, the soul and the Lord together is what he counts as real knowledge.",
  '13.4': "Krishna promises a concise account of the field: what it is, how it changes, where it comes from, and who its knower is along with that knower's powers. He prepares Arjuna for a systematic teaching.",
  '13.5': "Krishna grounds his teaching in tradition, noting that sages have described the field and its knower in many ways, in Vedic hymns and especially in the reasoned, conclusive aphorisms of the *Vedanta-sutra*.",
  '13.6': "Krishna begins cataloguing the components of the field, which include the physical elements, the sense of ego, intelligence, and the senses with their objects. His point is that both body and mind belong to material nature.",
  '13.7': "Completing the list, Krishna adds desire, aversion, pleasure, pain, the bodily aggregate, life signs and resolve. Even our likes, dislikes and emotions belong to the changing field, distinct from the self that observes them.",
  '13.9': "Adding to his description of real knowledge, Krishna highlights indifference to sense pleasure, humility, and steady awareness that birth, aging, illness and death bring misery. Seeing these facts honestly weakens worldly attachment.",
  '13.10': "Further marks of knowledge include freedom from clinging to children, spouse and home, and a steady mind that stays balanced whether pleasant or unpleasant things happen. Such balance reflects inner freedom.",
  '13.11': "Krishna places unwavering, exclusive devotion to himself at the heart of the qualities of knowledge, along with a taste for solitude and disinterest in crowds. Pure *bhakti* is the thread that holds the whole list together.",
  '13.12': "The list closes with constant attention to the self and insight into the purpose of knowing truth. Krishna declares all these qualities to be knowledge, and whatever runs contrary to them ignorance.",
  '13.13': "Krishna turns to the object of knowledge, whose realization brings immortality. He describes the beginningless *Brahman*, subordinate to him, which lies beyond the categories of cause and effect.",
  '13.14': "Krishna depicts the all-pervading Supreme as having hands, feet, eyes, heads, faces and ears everywhere. Present throughout the cosmos, the Lord perceives and responds to everything at once.",
  '13.15': "The verse presents a paradox: the Supreme is the source of all senses yet needs no material senses, unattached yet sustaining all, beyond the *gunas* yet their master and enjoyer. He transcends nature while upholding it.",
  '13.16': "Krishna explains that the Supreme exists both within and outside all beings, moving and unmoving. Being subtle, he escapes ordinary perception, so he seems far away to some while remaining very near.",
  '13.17': "The Supreme appears divided among countless beings yet remains undivided. Krishna describes him as the one who sustains all creatures, and who also brings them into being and later withdraws them.",
  '13.18': "Krishna calls the Supreme the light within all lights, beyond darkness. He is knowledge itself, the goal of knowledge and what knowledge reaches, and he dwells in every heart.",
  '13.19': "Krishna sums up his teaching on the field, knowledge and the knowable. A devotee who truly grasps this attains a nature like his own, showing that the purpose of knowledge is devotion.",
  '13.20': "Krishna teaches that both material nature and the living being are beginningless. All transformations of the body and the three *gunas* arise from nature, distinct from the conscious self.",
  '13.21': "Krishna divides responsibility: material nature produces bodies, senses and their actions, while the conscious self is the one who experiences pleasure and pain. The soul is the experiencer, nature the mechanism.",
  '13.22': "The living being, dwelling within material nature, tastes the qualities that nature produces. Its attachment to those *gunas* is what carries it into good and bad births again and again.",
  '13.23': "Krishna describes a second, higher person within the body: the Supersoul, who witnesses, permits, sustains and supervises as the great Lord. This shows the individual self is never alone in the body.",
  '13.24': "Whoever truly understands the living being, material nature and its qualities is freed from rebirth, whatever their present circumstances. Clear knowledge of these distinctions brings liberation.",
  '13.25': "Krishna acknowledges various paths to perceiving the Supersoul within: some through meditation, some through philosophical analysis, and others through selfless work. Each approach seeks the same inner reality.",
  '13.26': "Even those unfamiliar with these disciplines can cross beyond death by hearing from realized teachers and faithfully acting on what they hear. Krishna highlights the power of attentive hearing in spiritual life.",
  '13.27': "Krishna explains that every being that comes into existence, moving or still, arises from the union of the field and its knower. All life is a meeting of matter and spirit.",
  '13.28': "Real vision, Krishna says, is seeing the Supreme Lord present equally in all beings, the imperishable within the perishable. One who sees this way truly sees.",
  '13.29': "Seeing the Lord equally present everywhere, a person stops degrading themselves through their own mind and attains the highest destination. Spiritual vision protects the self from its own downfall.",
  '13.30': "One who sees that material nature performs all actions, and that the self is not the real doer, sees accurately. This insight frees a person from pride and anxiety over action.",
  '13.31': "When a person sees the variety of beings as resting in one source and expanding from it, they reach spiritual realization. Diversity is understood as rooted in a single reality.",
  '13.32': "Because it is beginningless and beyond the *gunas*, the imperishable Supreme Self remains untouched while dwelling in the body. It neither acts in a binding way nor becomes entangled.",
  '13.33': "Krishna compares the self to space, which pervades everything yet is never stained because it is so subtle. In the same way the self within the body remains unaffected by it.",
  '13.34': "Just as one sun illuminates the whole world, the knower of the field illuminates the entire body with consciousness. Awareness spreading through the body is the sign of the soul's presence.",
  '13.35': "The chapter concludes that those who use the eye of wisdom to see the difference between the field and its knower, and the way out of material nature, reach the supreme goal.",
  '14.1': "Krishna offers to explain again the highest of all knowledge, by which sages in the past attained ultimate perfection. He signals that the teaching on the *gunas* that follows is especially valuable.",
  '14.2': "Those who take shelter of this knowledge come to share a nature like Krishna's. They are no longer born at creation nor disturbed at cosmic dissolution, having passed beyond the cycle.",
  '14.3': "Krishna describes vast material nature as a womb into which he places the seed of life. From this union all beings come into existence, revealing him as the origin behind nature.",
  '14.4': "Krishna calls himself the seed-giving father of all forms of life, with material nature as their mother. Every species, in every womb, ultimately traces back to him.",
  '14.5': "Krishna introduces the three *gunas*, goodness, passion and ignorance, which arise from nature. They bind the eternal, unchanging soul to the body, explaining how a spiritual being becomes conditioned.",
  '14.6': "Goodness is pure, illuminating and free from distress, yet it still binds. Krishna shows that even attachment to happiness and knowledge keeps the soul tied to material existence.",
  '14.7': "Passion is born of craving and attachment and is full of longing. Krishna explains that it binds the embodied soul by tying it to restless action and its results.",
  '14.8': "Ignorance arises from lack of knowledge and deludes all embodied beings. Krishna says it binds through carelessness, laziness and excessive sleep, dulling awareness of the self.",
  '14.9': "Krishna summarizes how each *guna* binds: goodness attaches one to happiness, passion to action, and ignorance, by covering knowledge, to negligence. Each quality has its own grip.",
  '14.10': "The three *gunas* compete constantly. Krishna explains that goodness rises by overpowering passion and ignorance, and each of the others prevails in the same way, so a person's state shifts over time.",
  '14.11': "Krishna explains how to recognize goodness: it prevails when knowledge illuminates every gateway of the body. Clear perception through the senses and mind is the mark of this mode at work.",
  '14.12': "When passion increases, Krishna says, greed, intense activity, ambitious undertakings, restlessness and longing appear. These symptoms help one recognize passion in oneself.",
  '14.13': "When ignorance grows, darkness, inertia, negligence and delusion take hold. Krishna offers these symptoms so a seeker can notice when ignorance is clouding the mind.",
  '14.14': "One who dies when goodness predominates reaches the pure worlds of those who know the highest truth. The quality active at death shapes the next destination.",
  '14.15': "Dying in passion leads to birth among those attached to work, while dying in ignorance leads to birth in deluded forms of life. The *gunas* steer the soul's next birth.",
  '14.16': "Krishna contrasts the fruits of action: virtuous action in goodness yields purity, action in passion brings suffering, and action in ignorance leads to deeper ignorance. Results follow the quality behind the act.",
  '14.17': "From goodness knowledge arises, from passion greed, and from ignorance negligence, delusion and lack of understanding. Each *guna* produces its own mental results.",
  '14.18': "Those established in goodness rise upward, those in passion remain in the middle, and those in ignorance sink downward. Krishna maps the soul's movement through the levels of existence.",
  '14.19': "When a seer understands that the *gunas* are the only doers and knows what lies beyond them, that person attains Krishna's own nature. Insight into the qualities opens the way past them.",
  '14.20': "Krishna teaches that when the soul rises above the three *gunas*, the source of bodily existence, it escapes the miseries of birth, aging and death. Such a soul then enjoys the nectar of immortality.",
  '14.21': "Arjuna asks Krishna three practical questions about one who has gone beyond the *gunas*: what are their signs, how do they behave, and how do they rise above the three qualities.",
  '14.23': "Krishna describes the transcendent person as sitting like a neutral observer, unshaken by the *gunas*. Knowing that the qualities are simply acting, they remain steady and unmoved.",
  '14.24': "Such a person is equal in sorrow and joy, settled in the self, and sees a clod, a stone and gold alike. Steady and wise, they treat pleasant and unpleasant, praise and blame, the same.",
  '14.25': "Krishna describes someone who has risen above the *gunas*: praise and contempt affect them equally, allies and opponents are treated alike, and they have given up projects driven by personal ambition.",
  '14.26': "Krishna answers how to rise beyond the *gunas*: by serving him with unwavering devotion. Such *bhakti* carries a person past the three qualities to the spiritual platform.",
  '14.27': "Krishna declares himself the foundation of *Brahman*, of immortal and imperishable existence, of eternal righteousness and of ultimate happiness. All spiritual attainments rest on him.",
  '15.1': "Krishna describes the material world as an imperishable banyan tree with roots above and branches below, its leaves being the Vedic hymns. Whoever understands this tree understands the Vedas.",
  '15.2': "The tree's branches spread up and down, nourished by the *gunas*, with sense objects as its twigs. Its roots extend downward too, binding people to action in the human world.",
  '15.3': "The true form of this tree, its beginning, end and foundation, cannot be perceived from within the world. Krishna urges cutting its deep roots with the strong weapon of detachment.",
  '15.4': "After cutting the tree, one must seek the place from which there is no return, surrendering to the original Person from whom all creation has flowed since ancient times.",
  '15.5': "Those free from pride and delusion, who have conquered attachment, stay devoted to the self, set aside lust and are released from the dualities of pleasure and pain reach the eternal abode.",
  '15.6': "Krishna's own realm shines by its own light, so sun, moon and fire play no role there. He teaches that whoever arrives in that eternal home never comes back to material existence.",
  '15.8': "The living being carries its mind and senses from one body to the next, just as the wind carries scents from their source. The self remains while bodies change.",
  '15.9': "Taking up a new body, the soul presides over ears, eyes, touch, taste, smell and the mind, and through them enjoys sense objects. The senses are the self's instruments of experience.",
  '15.10': "The deluded fail to perceive the soul as it leaves the body, stays in it or experiences the *gunas*. Only those with the eye of knowledge can see it.",
  '15.11': "Dedicated yogis who strive can perceive the self established within. Those whose minds are unrefined cannot see it, however hard they try, showing that inner purification matters.",
  '15.12': "Krishna reveals that the radiance of the sun lighting the world, and the glow of the moon and fire, all come from him. Every source of light reflects his splendor.",
  '15.13': "Entering the earth, Krishna sustains all beings with his energy. As the moon, which carries the essence of taste, he nourishes all plants, revealing his care in natural processes.",
  '15.14': "Krishna explains that he becomes the digestive fire in living bodies, joining with the vital breaths to digest four kinds of food. Even bodily functions depend on him.",
  '15.15': "Krishna declares that he dwells in every heart and is the source of memory, knowledge and forgetfulness. He is the goal of all the Vedas, the author of Vedanta and its true knower.",
  '15.16': "Krishna describes two kinds of persons: the fallible, which includes all beings in the material world, and the infallible, those established in an unchanging spiritual state.",
  '15.17': "Beyond both of these is the highest Person, called the Supersoul. As the imperishable Lord, he enters the three worlds and sustains them.",
  '15.18': "Krishna explains why he bears the title *Purushottama*: he stands beyond perishable matter and is greater even than the imperishable souls. For this reason both the world and the Vedas honor him as the highest person.",
  '15.19': "Whoever knows Krishna as the Supreme Person, free from confusion, knows everything worth knowing and worships him with their whole being. Full knowledge culminates in devotion.",
  '15.20': "Krishna calls this teaching the most confidential part of scripture. Understanding it makes a person wise and brings their efforts to fulfillment, marking it as the essence of the Vedas.",
  '16.2': "Krishna continues describing the divine nature, highlighting virtues such as nonviolence and compassion toward all creatures. Taken together, these gentle and steady qualities support a person's spiritual growth.",
  '16.3': "Krishna completes the divine qualities with vigor, forgiveness, fortitude, cleanliness, freedom from malice and absence of pride. These traits belong to one born with a godly nature.",
  '16.4': "Krishna sketches the demonic temperament through traits such as pride and harsh speech, alongside anger and ignorance. These qualities, he tells Arjuna, belong to those born with a nature opposed to the divine.",
  '16.5': "Divine qualities lead to liberation, while demoniac ones lead to bondage. Krishna reassures Arjuna not to worry, since he was born with a divine nature.",
  '16.6': "Krishna explains that there are two kinds of beings in the world, the divine and the demoniac. Having described the divine at length, he now turns to the demoniac.",
  '16.7': "Those of demoniac nature do not know what should be done or avoided. Krishna says they lack cleanliness, proper conduct and truthfulness, having lost basic moral orientation.",
  '16.8': "The demoniac claim the world is unreal, without foundation and without a God. They see it as arising from mere combination, driven by nothing but lust.",
  '16.9': "Holding this view, the lost and small-minded engage in cruel deeds. Krishna warns that such people become enemies of the world and work toward its destruction.",
  '16.10': "Driven by insatiable desire and full of hypocrisy, pride and arrogance, the demoniac cling to false ideas out of delusion and pursue impure aims.",
  '16.11': "The demoniac are consumed by endless anxieties that last until death. Treating sense gratification as life's highest goal, they are convinced there is nothing more.",
  '16.12': "Bound by hundreds of hopes and devoted to lust and anger, the demoniac pile up wealth by unjust means to feed their desires. Craving becomes a cage.",
  '16.13': "Krishna describes the demoniac mindset: always counting what they have gained today, what they will get next, and how much more wealth will be theirs. Acquisition becomes endless.",
  '16.14': "The demoniac boast of killing one enemy and planning to kill others. They imagine themselves as lord and enjoyer, perfect, powerful and happy, revealing the depth of their pride.",
  '16.15': "Proud of wealth and family, the demoniac think no one equals them and plan rituals and charity only for their own enjoyment. Deluded and entangled in desire, they fall into a dark condition.",
  '16.16': "Confused by many thoughts and caught in a web of delusion, those attached to sense pleasures fall into a hellish condition. Unchecked craving leads to degradation.",
  '16.17': "Self-important, stubborn and intoxicated by wealth and status, the demoniac perform sacrifices in name only, with hypocrisy and without following scripture. Their religion serves their ego.",
  '16.18': "Krishna describes how demonic people, leaning on ego, strength and rage, grow resentful and spiteful. Their hostility targets the Lord himself, who dwells as the Supersoul within their own hearts and within everyone else.",
  '16.19': "Krishna says that those who are envious, cruel and the lowest of humanity are repeatedly cast into demoniac births within the cycle of rebirth. Their nature determines their destination.",
  '16.20': "Taking birth after birth in demoniac forms, the deluded never reach Krishna and sink to ever lower conditions. Rejecting God leads to deepening degradation.",
  '16.21': "Krishna names three gates to hell that destroy the self: lust, anger and greed. He urges that all three be abandoned, since they are the roots of downfall.",
  '16.22': "Whoever escapes these three gates of darkness acts for their own true welfare and reaches the highest destination. Freedom from lust, anger and greed opens the path upward.",
  '16.23': "Krishna cautions against discarding the rules of scripture to follow personal impulse. Someone who lives that way reaches no real fulfillment, no lasting joy, and no spiritual destination.",
  '16.24': "Krishna concludes that scripture should be the authority in deciding what to do and avoid. Knowing its guidance, Arjuna should act accordingly in this world.",
  '17.1': "Arjuna asks Krishna about people who set aside scriptural rules yet still worship with sincere faith. He wants to know which mode of nature governs their commitment: goodness, passion or ignorance.",
  '17.2': "Krishna answers that faith in every embodied being takes one of three forms, shaped by the person's own nature. He prepares to describe faith in goodness, passion and ignorance in turn.",
  '17.4': "Krishna explains that the object of worship reveals a person's mode. Those in goodness worship the gods, those in passion worship powerful spirits and demons, and those in ignorance turn to ghosts and the spirits of the dead.",
  '17.5': "Krishna describes people who perform severe austerities that scripture never prescribes. Driven by hypocrisy, ego, lust and attachment, their harsh practices come from pride and craving and serve no genuine spiritual aim.",
  '17.6': "Such misguided ascetics torment the elements of their own bodies and also trouble Krishna, who dwells within as the Supersoul. Krishna calls their resolve demonic, since it harms both body and the Lord within.",
  '17.7': "Krishna turns to everyday life, saying that the food people like also falls into three kinds, as do sacrifice, austerity and charity. He invites Arjuna to hear how the modes distinguish each of these.",
  '17.8': "Foods dear to those in goodness are juicy, wholesome, nourishing and pleasing to the heart. Krishna notes that they increase life, clarity, strength, health, happiness and contentment, linking diet with inner purity.",
  '17.9': "Krishna says people in passion favor foods that are too bitter, sour, salty, hot, pungent, dry or burning. Such food brings pain, distress and disease, showing how craving for intensity leads to suffering.",
  '17.10': "Food liked by those in ignorance is stale, tasteless, putrid, left over from others or impure. Krishna presents such eating as a sign of dullness, since it nourishes neither body nor mind.",
  '17.11': "Sacrifice in goodness is offered according to scripture, simply because it ought to be done, by people who want no reward. The verse teaches that a settled sense of duty purifies the act of worship.",
  '17.12': "Krishna explains that sacrifice performed for some material gain, or to make a show of piety, belongs to passion. Ambition and display spoil an offering that could otherwise purify the giver.",
  '17.13': "Sacrifice in ignorance ignores scriptural direction, distributes no food, omits the sacred hymns, gives nothing to the priests and lacks faith. Krishna treats such careless ritual as empty of any real spiritual value.",
  '17.14': "Krishna defines austerity of the body: honoring the gods, the twice-born, teachers and the wise, along with cleanliness, straightforwardness, celibacy and nonviolence. Physical discipline is rooted in respect and restraint.",
  '17.15': "Austerity of speech means words that cause no agitation, that are truthful, kind and beneficial, together with regular recitation of sacred texts. Krishna shows that disciplined speech is itself a spiritual practice.",
  '17.16': "Austerity of mind consists of serenity, gentleness, silence, self-control and purity of intention. Krishna presents inner calm and clean motives as the deepest layer of discipline.",
  '17.17': "When these three austerities of body, speech and mind are practiced with deep faith by steady people who seek no reward, Krishna calls them austerity in goodness.",
  '17.18': "Austerity performed with pretense to win respect, honor and reverence belongs to passion. Krishna says such discipline is unstable and fleeting, because it depends on the approval of others.",
  '17.19': "Austerity in ignorance comes from foolish stubbornness, involves torturing oneself, or aims to injure someone else. Krishna marks it as the lowest kind, rooted in delusion and harm.",
  '17.20': "Charity in goodness is given because giving is right, to a worthy recipient at a suitable place and time, with no expectation of return. Krishna highlights the purity of selfless, well-placed generosity.",
  '17.21': "Charity given reluctantly, in hope of a favor in return or for some future benefit, belongs to passion. Krishna shows how calculation and grudging attitudes reduce the worth of a gift.",
  '17.22': "Charity in ignorance is given at the wrong place or time to unworthy people, without respect and with contempt. Krishna teaches that how and to whom one gives matters as much as the gift.",
  '17.23': "Krishna explains that the threefold phrase om tat sat has long been used to point to the Absolute Truth. Through it the priestly order, the Vedas and sacrifice were established at the beginning.",
  '17.24': "For this reason, followers of the Vedas always begin their prescribed sacrifices, gifts and austerities by uttering om. The sacred syllable connects every religious act with the Supreme.",
  '17.25': "Seekers of liberation perform sacrifice, austerity and many kinds of charity while saying tat, without desiring any fruit. The word reminds them that their acts belong to the Supreme and aim at freedom.",
  '17.26': "Krishna explains that the word sat denotes both eternal reality and goodness. It is also used for any praiseworthy deed, signaling that the act is aligned with the Absolute Truth.",
  '17.27': "Steadiness in sacrifice, austerity and charity is also called sat, as is any action done for the sake of the Supreme. Work offered to the Lord gains the quality of eternal reality.",
  '17.28': "Krishna concludes that any sacrifice, gift or austerity done without faith is called asat, unreal. It brings no benefit in this life or the next, making faith the essential ingredient of spiritual practice.",
  '18.1': "Opening the final chapter, Arjuna asks Krishna to explain the true nature of *sannyasa*, renunciation, and of tyaga, relinquishment, and how the two differ from each other.",
  '18.2': "Krishna reports that the wise call giving up desire-driven actions renunciation, while discerning thinkers define relinquishment as giving up the fruits of all actions. The distinction turns on motive.",
  '18.3': "Krishna notes that some thinkers say all action should be abandoned as inherently flawed, while others insist that sacrifice, charity and austerity must never be given up. He sets out the debate before resolving it.",
  '18.4': "Krishna asks Arjuna to hear his own settled conclusion about relinquishment. He states that relinquishment is of three kinds, preparing to classify it by the modes of nature.",
  '18.5': "Krishna rules that sacrifice, charity and austerity should never be abandoned and must be performed. These acts purify even the wise, so they remain valuable at every stage of spiritual life.",
  '18.6': "Even these purifying acts should be done without attachment and without desire for results. Krishna calls this his final and best judgment, defining true relinquishment as detachment within action.",
  '18.7': "Krishna teaches that obligatory duties are not to be dropped. Someone who abandons them through delusion is practicing a kind of renunciation that he classifies under the mode of ignorance.",
  '18.8': "Krishna calls it renunciation in passion when someone quits a duty because it feels painful or physically demanding. Giving up work for comfort's sake, he says, brings none of the real benefit renunciation promises.",
  '18.9': "When a person performs prescribed duty simply because it should be done, giving up attachment and the desire for its fruit, Krishna calls that relinquishment in goodness. This is the model he recommends.",
  '18.10': "The wise renouncer, steeped in goodness and free of doubt, neither resents unpleasant work nor clings to pleasant work. Krishna describes an even-minded person who acts without preference or aversion.",
  '18.11': "Krishna points out that no embodied being can give up action entirely. Therefore the true renouncer is one who gives up the fruits of action, making detached work the practical path of renunciation.",
  '18.12': "Those who do not renounce the fruits of action receive three kinds of results after death: desirable, undesirable and mixed. Real renunciants are untouched by such reactions, being free from karmic bondage.",
  '18.13': "Krishna asks Arjuna to learn the five causes behind the accomplishment of every action, as taught in the analytical philosophy of Sankhya. He prepares to explain how action really comes about.",
  '18.14': "Krishna names the five factors of action: the body as the seat, the doer, the various senses as instruments, the many kinds of effort, and the divine will, with the Supersoul as the final factor.",
  '18.15': "Whatever a person begins with body, speech or mind, whether right or wrong, arises from these five factors. Krishna shows that no action rests on the individual alone.",
  '18.16': "Knowing these five causes, one who still sees himself as the only doer lacks understanding. Krishna says such a person, with an untrained intellect, fails to see things as they truly are.",
  '18.17': "One free from the false ego of doership, whose intelligence is untainted, does not truly kill and is not bound, even when slaying in battle. Krishna links freedom from ego with freedom from karmic reaction.",
  '18.18': "Krishna breaks action into two triads. What sets action in motion is knowing, the thing to be known and the one who knows; what carries it out is the doer, the deed and the means.",
  '18.19': "Krishna announces that knowledge, action and the doer are each of three kinds according to the modes of nature, as described in the science of the gunas. He invites Arjuna to hear them.",
  '18.20': "Knowledge in goodness sees one undivided, imperishable spiritual reality in all beings, even amid their many outward forms. Krishna presents this as perceiving the common spiritual essence in every living being.",
  '18.21': "Knowledge in passion sees only separateness, regarding each body as a distinct kind of being with nothing deeper connecting them. Krishna marks this view as fragmented and shallow.",
  '18.22': "Knowledge in ignorance clings to one small thing as if it were everything, without reason or grasp of truth. Krishna describes it as narrow, baseless and blind to reality.",
  '18.23': "Krishna explains that work belongs to the mode of goodness when it is a regulated duty carried out free of attachment, likes and dislikes, by someone who wants nothing in return. It is selfless, balanced action.",
  '18.24': "Action in passion is undertaken with great strain by someone hungry for desires or driven by ego. Krishna shows how ambition makes work exhausting and self-centered.",
  '18.25': "Action in ignorance is started out of delusion, ignoring consequences, loss, harm to others and one's own ability. Krishna identifies reckless, careless action as the lowest kind.",
  '18.26': "The doer in goodness is free of attachment and egotism, full of steadiness and enthusiasm, and unchanged by success or failure. Krishna portrays an agent who works with resolve and equanimity.",
  '18.27': "The doer in passion is attached, eager for the fruits of work, greedy, violent, impure and tossed between joy and sorrow. Krishna depicts an agent ruled by desire and swings of emotion.",
  '18.28': "The doer in ignorance is undisciplined, vulgar, stubborn, deceitful, malicious, lazy, gloomy and given to procrastination. Krishna lists these traits as marks of a worker clouded by tamas.",
  '18.29': "Krishna now offers to describe in full detail the three kinds of intelligence and of determination, each classified according to the modes of nature.",
  '18.30': "Intelligence in goodness knows when to act and when to refrain, what should and should not be done, what to fear and not fear, and what binds and what liberates. Krishna calls this clear discernment.",
  '18.31': "Intelligence in passion cannot clearly tell right from wrong, or duty from what should be avoided. Krishna describes a mind that sees moral choices in a blurred and confused way.",
  '18.32': "Intelligence in ignorance, wrapped in darkness, takes wrong for right and sees everything backward. Krishna presents it as total inversion of understanding.",
  '18.33': "Determination in goodness is unwavering resolve, sustained through yoga, that holds the mind, breath and senses steady. Krishna presents it as disciplined steadiness directed toward the spiritual goal.",
  '18.34': "Determination in passion holds tightly to duty, pleasure and wealth, with attachment and a desire for their rewards. Krishna shows resolve driven by the hope of gain.",
  '18.35': "Determination in ignorance is the dull persistence by which a foolish person refuses to let go of sleep, fear, grief, depression and arrogance. Krishna shows stubbornness serving one's own darkness.",
  '18.36': "Krishna now introduces the three kinds of happiness. He speaks of a joy that grows through steady practice and in which a person eventually reaches the end of suffering.",
  '18.37': "Happiness in goodness seems like poison at first but becomes like nectar in the end. Krishna says it springs from the clarity of a mind that knows the self, rewarding patient discipline.",
  '18.38': "Happiness in passion arises from contact between the senses and their objects. It tastes like nectar at first and turns to poison in the end, revealing the hidden cost of sensual pleasure.",
  '18.39': "Happiness in ignorance deludes the self from beginning to end. Krishna says it comes from sleep, laziness and negligence, offering only a false comfort that keeps a person in darkness.",
  '18.40': "Krishna states that not a single being, whether a human on this planet or a demigod in the heavens, escapes the influence of the three qualities that nature produces. Every level of material existence is under their sway.",
  '18.41': "Krishna explains that the duties of the brahmanas, kshatriyas, vaishyas and shudras are divided according to qualities arising from each person's nature. Social roles reflect inborn temperament.",
  '18.42': "Krishna lists the natural qualities that suit a brahmana: peacefulness, self-control, austerity, purity, patience, honesty, knowledge, wisdom and faith in God. These shape a life of spiritual guidance.",
  '18.43': "Heroism, strength, determination, skill, courage in battle, generosity and the capacity to lead are the natural qualities of a kshatriya. Krishna reminds Arjuna of the warrior's inborn duty.",
  '18.44': "Farming, protecting cows and trade are the natural work of the vaishya, while service is the natural work of the shudra. Krishna presents each kind of labor as a valid expression of one's nature.",
  '18.45': "Krishna teaches that a person who is devoted to their own natural work attains perfection. He offers to explain how dedication to one's duty leads to that fulfilment.",
  '18.47': "Krishna says it is better to perform one's own *dharma* imperfectly than another's duty well. Acting according to one's nature incurs no sin, which supports Arjuna's duty to fight.",
  '18.48': "Krishna advises against abandoning the work suited to one's nature just because it has flaws. Just as smoke always accompanies fire, some fault clings to every effort, so imperfect duty is still worth doing.",
  '18.49': "One whose intelligence is unattached everywhere, who has mastered the self and is free from cravings, attains the highest perfection of freedom from karmic reaction through renunciation. Krishna crowns detached work.",
  '18.50': "Krishna offers to explain briefly how one who has reached this perfection attains the realization of Brahman, which he calls the highest stage of knowledge.",
  '18.51': "Krishna begins describing the path to Brahman realization: a purified intellect, steady self-control, withdrawal from sense objects like sound, and the setting aside of attachment and aversion.",
  '18.52': "The seeker lives in solitude, eats lightly, restrains speech, body and mind, stays absorbed in meditation and takes shelter in detachment. Krishna continues outlining the disciplined life leading to Brahman.",
  '18.53': "Having given up ego, force, pride, lust, anger and possessiveness, free from the sense of ownership and at peace, a person becomes fit for Brahman realization. Krishna completes the list of qualifications.",
  '18.54': "One fixed in Brahman is joyful, neither grieving nor craving, and sees all beings equally. Krishna says such a person then attains supreme devotion to him, showing that bhakti lies beyond Brahman realization.",
  '18.55': "Krishna teaches that only through devotion can one truly know him as he is. Having known him in truth, the devotee enters into his presence, making *bhakti* the doorway to complete knowledge of God.",
  '18.56': "Krishna promises that a devotee who depends on him reaches his eternal, unchanging abode by his grace, even while busy with every kind of work. Devotion turns ordinary activity into a path home.",
  '18.57': "Krishna urges Arjuna to mentally offer all actions to him, regard him as the supreme goal, rely on the yoga of intelligence and keep his mind always absorbed in him.",
  '18.58': "Krishna promises that by fixing his mind on him, Arjuna will cross every obstacle by divine grace. If he ignores this counsel out of ego, however, he will be ruined.",
  '18.59': "Krishna warns that if Arjuna, relying on ego, resolves not to fight, the decision is useless. His own warrior nature will compel him into action anyway.",
  '18.60': "Krishna tells Arjuna that he is bound by the duty born of his own nature. What he refuses to do out of confusion, he will end up doing helplessly, driven by that nature.",
  '18.61': "Krishna reveals that the Lord resides in the heart of every being, directing their wanderings as if they were mounted on a machine made of material energy. The Supersoul oversees every life.",
  '18.62': "Krishna urges Arjuna to take refuge in the Lord with his whole being. By that grace he will attain supreme peace and the eternal abode, making surrender the path to lasting shelter.",
  '18.63': "Krishna says he has now shared knowledge more confidential than the confidential. He asks Arjuna to reflect on it fully and then choose freely what to do, honoring Arjuna's free will.",
  '18.64': "Krishna offers to speak once more his most confidential and supreme instruction. He shares it for Arjuna's benefit because Arjuna is very dear to him, revealing the loving basis of the teaching.",
  '18.65': "Krishna asks Arjuna to keep his mind on him, become his devotee, worship him and bow to him. He promises that Arjuna will surely come to him, because Arjuna is dear to him.",
  '18.67': "Krishna instructs that this confidential teaching should never be shared with those lacking austerity, those without devotion, those unwilling to listen, or those who resent him. The message requires a receptive heart.",
  '18.68': "Krishna promises that whoever teaches this deepest secret to his devotees is offering him the highest devotion. Such a teacher will certainly reach him, showing how much he values anyone who passes the Gita on.",
  '18.69': "No one in human society is dearer to Krishna or does him greater service than one who teaches this message to devotees. Krishna says none ever will be dearer to him on earth.",
  '18.70': "Krishna declares that whoever studies this sacred conversation between them worships him through the sacrifice of knowledge. Studying the Gita itself becomes an act of devotion.",
  '18.71': "Even one who simply listens to this teaching with faith and without envy is freed from sinful reactions. Krishna says such a person reaches the auspicious worlds of the virtuous.",
  '18.72': "Krishna asks Arjuna whether he has heard all this with full attention and whether his confusion and ignorance have now been dispelled. He checks that the teaching has truly reached him.",
  '18.73': "Arjuna tells Krishna that by his mercy the illusion has vanished and true memory has returned. Standing firm with all doubts removed, he declares he will carry out Krishna's instructions, completing his transformation.",
  '18.74': "Sanjaya tells King Dhritarashtra that he has heard this wondrous dialogue between Krishna and the great-souled Arjuna. The conversation has thrilled him so much that his hair stands on end.",
  '18.75': "Sanjaya explains that by the grace of his teacher Vyasa he heard this supreme secret of yoga directly from Krishna, the master of all yoga, as Krishna himself was speaking.",
  '18.76': "Sanjaya tells the king that as he remembers again and again this holy and wondrous dialogue between Krishna and Arjuna, he feels joy over and over.",
  '18.77': "Sanjaya says that recalling again and again the astonishing form of Krishna fills him with great wonder and repeated joy. The memory of the Lord's form keeps his devotion alive.",
};
